e previously chosen sunny lines cover every point of the original set.  \n\n   The construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, giving a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines. Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nSince the lower bound and the construction match, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|T_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary condition: at least two lines are non‑sunny  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct covering lines at most one is non‑sunny.  \nThen all but possibly one of the lines are sunny, i.e. have slopes different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Claim.* A sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and\n\\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\).  \nSince both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb_{2}-b_{1}=-(a_{2}-a_{1})\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nor \\(a_{2}=a_{1}\\) (vertical) or \\(b_{2}=b_{1}\\) (horizontal).  \nThus the slope would be \\(-1\\), \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of a sunny line. ∎  \n\nConsequently, if at most one line is non‑sunny, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n1\\cdot |T_{n}|_{\\text{by the non‑sunny line}}+(n-1)\\cdot 1\\le |T_{n}|,\n\\]\n\nbut a single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the\nvertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points with that fixed \\(c\\), etc.).  \nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), the covering would be\nincomplete – a contradiction.  \n\nHence **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Choosing the sunny lines  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) that passes through \\(P_{i}\\) and whose slope is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the other\n\\(\\ell_{j}\\) (\\(j\\neq i\\)).  \nBecause the set of forbidden slopes is finite, such a choice is always possible,\nand the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nThus we have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of\n\\(T_{n}\\) (the point \\(P_{i}\\) itself).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}=T_{n}\\setminus\\{P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T'_{n}\\) is the triangular lattice of size \\(n-k\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\;a\\neq 1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Covering the remaining points with non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following \\(n-k\\) lines, all of which are non‑sunny:\n\n* **Vertical lines**  \n  \\[\n  V_{c}: x=c\\qquad\\text{for }c=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-k-2\\) such lines (the range contains \\(n-1-(k+1)+1=n-k-1\\) values,\n  and we will omit the last one).\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H: y=1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\) line**  \n  \\[\n  D: x+y=n-k+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAll three families are pairwise distinct and none coincides with any of the\nsunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) because the latter have slopes different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(T'_{n}\\) lies on at least one of these\n\\(n-k\\) lines.\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T'_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(b=1\\), it lies on\n  the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\), then \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-2\\); consequently\n  \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the point lies on the vertical line\n  \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) (which cannot happen because those \\(a\\) have been removed) the\n  point would be one of the \\(P_{i}\\); thus the only remaining case is\n  \\(a+b=n-k+1\\), i.e. the point lies on the line \\(D\\).\n\nHence every element of \\(T'_{n}\\) is covered by at least one of the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  Counting  \n\nWe have produced:\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(V_{c}\\;(c=k+1,\\dots ,n-1),\\;H,\\;D\\).\n\nAll these lines are distinct, and together they cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited a configuration satisfying the\nrequirements.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\nthe inequality (1) is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for every\n\\(k\\) in that range show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for\nwhich such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).**"
