ince \\(|T_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), the covering would be\nincomplete – a contradiction.  \n\nHence **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Choosing the sunny lines  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) that passes through \\(P_{i}\\) and whose slope is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the other\n\\(\\ell_{j}\\) (\\(j\\neq i\\)).  \nBecause the set of forbidden slopes is finite, such a choice is always possible,\nand the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nThus we have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of\n\\(T_{n}\\) (the point \\(P_{i}\\) itself).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}=T_{n}\\setminus\\{P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T'_{n}\\) is the triangular lattice of size \\(n-k\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\;a\\neq 1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Covering the remaining points with non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following \\(n-k\\) lines, all of which are non‑sunny:\n\n* **Vertical lines**  \n  \\[\n  V_{c}: x=c\\qquad\\text{for }c=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-k-2\\) such lines (the range contains \\(n-1-(k+1)+1=n-k-1\\) values,\n  and we will omit the last one).\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H: y=1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\) line**  \n  \\[\n  D: x+y=n-k+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAll three families are pairwise distinct and none coincides with any of the\nsunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) because the latter have slopes different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n