=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal C_{s}=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n\\) possible \\(x\\)-values, \\(n\\) possible \\(y\\)-values and \\(n\\) possible sums\n\\(x+y\\) (the sums run from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)).  \nBecause a sunny line cannot use the same value twice in any of the three families,\nthe number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can lie on a sunny line is at most \\(n-1\\):\nif a sunny line contained \\(n\\) points, then by the pigeon\u2011hole principle two of them\nwould share either an \\(x\\)-coordinate, a \\(y\\)-coordinate or a sum, contradicting Lemma\u202f1.\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_{n}\\text{ in at most }n-1\\text{ points.}} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow suppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); each such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the whole line segment inside the triangle has length \\(n\\) in the lattice). Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).\n\nUsing (1), the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k(n-1)\\) points.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}| \\le (n-k)n + k(n-1)= n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **at least two of the \\(n\\) covering lines must be non\u2011sunny**, and any admissible\n\\(k\\) satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Sunny lines  \n\nSelect the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nwhich all belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) that\n\n* passes through \\(P_{i}\\);\n* has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n* has a slope different from the slopes already used for \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i-1}\\).\n\nOnly three slopes are forbidden, so such a choice is always possible; the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct and are sunny.  \nEach \\(\\ell_{i}\\) covers exactly the point \\(P_{i}\\) (it may meet other points of \\(T_{n}\\), but those points will also be covered later, so no conflict arises).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}=T_{n}\\setminus\\{P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nExplicitly  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T'_{n}\\) is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of \u201csize\u2019\u2019 \\(n-k\\); its cardinality is\n\\(|T'_{n}|=\\binom{n-k+1}{2}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(T'_{n}\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n-k\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n\n* **Vertical lines**  \n  \\[\n  V_{c}: x=c\\qquad\\text{for }c=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-k-2\\) such lines (the index set has size \\(n-1-(k+1)+1=n-k-1\\); we will later omit the last one).\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H: y=1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\) line**  \n  \\[\n  D: x+y=n-k+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAll three families are distinct from each other and from the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) because the latter have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(T'_{n}\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\in T'_{n}\\) satisfies \\(b=1\\)*, then by definition it lies on \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-2\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (by definition of \\(T'_{n}\\)), the integer \\(a\\) belongs to the set\n\\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n-k+1\\)*, the point lies on the line \\(D\\).  \n(The equality \\(a+b=n-k+1\\) can occur only when \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\), so the point has already been covered either by a vertical line or by \\(D\\); in any case it is covered.)\n\nThus every element of \\(T'_{n}\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  Counting  \n\nWe have produced\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(V_{c}\\;(c=k+1,\\dots ,n-1),\\;H,\\;D\\).\n\nAll are distinct, and together they cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).  \nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all those values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition derived in (2) gives \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) can indeed be realised. Therefore the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
