ints, has slope \\(\\frac{1}{n-2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (for \\(n\\ge3\\)), and therefore is sunny.  \n   After removing \\(D\\) and adding \\(S_D\\) the point \\((2,n-1)\\) (previously covered only by \\(D\\)) is now covered by \\(S_D\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) remains covered by the horizontal line \\(H_1\\). All other points are untouched, so the covering condition is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n   – **Replacing a horizontal line \\(H_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\).**  \n   The points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((2,c-1)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y-1=\\frac{c-1}{c-2}\\,(x-2).\n   \\]\n   Its slope \\(\\dfrac{c-1}{c-2}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every integer \\(c\\ge3\\); hence \\(S_c\\) is sunny.  \n   The point \\((c,1)\\) (previously covered only by \\(H_c\\)) is now covered by \\(S_c\\); the point \\((2,c-1)\\) is already covered by the diagonal \\(D\\) (or, after \\(D\\) has been replaced, by the sunny line \\(S_D\\)). Thus the covering property is maintained while the number of sunny lines grows by one.\n\n   By performing independently any subset of the replacements described above we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary, and the constructions above show that it is also sufficient. Hence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe covering condition of the problem requires that every point of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) given lines.  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of  \n\\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nSuppose a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThen the vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are integer multiples of the same direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and \\((p,q)\\) not proportional to \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are non‑zero.  \nBecause the three points lie in the triangle bounded by the lines \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\), the differences \\(|a_i-a_j|\\) and \\(|b_i-b_j|\\) are at most \\(n-1\\).  \nIf \\(|p|\\ge2\\) then moving from one lattice point to another along the line would skip at least one lattice point, contradicting the fact that all three points belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(|p|=|q|=1\\), which forces the direction to be \\((1,1)\\) (slope \\(1\\)) or \\((1,-1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe latter is excluded by the definition of sunny; the former would make the line parallel to \\(x-y=0\\), which is not forbidden, but a line of slope \\(1\\) intersecting the triangle can contain at most two lattice points of \\(T_n\\) (the two extreme points on the line segment).  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line covers }\\le 2\\text{ points of }T_n.}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points on a non‑sunny line  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=t\\)).  \nFor \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at least three such points because \\(n+1-c\\ge3\\).  \nSimilarly, the vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) contains at least three points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le 2n-3\\) the intersection with \\(T_n\\) consists of the points \\((a,t-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le t-1\\) and \\(t-a\\le n+1-a\\); this set has at least three elements.  \n(The two extreme diagonals \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=2n-2\\) contain only one point each and will never be used in the constructions below.)\n\nThus any non‑sunny line that we shall employ contains **at least three** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every non‑sunny line used below contains }\\ge 3\\text{ points of }T_n.}\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy (2) the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by (3) the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at least \\(3(n-k)\\) points.  \nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\) points must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n2k+3\\,(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n##### 4.1 Base configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& H_i:\\; y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n& V:\\; x=1,\\\\\n& D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none is sunny.  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n  – For \\(a=1\\) the point is on the vertical line \\(V