"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper bound:** Trivially \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n2. **Base configuration (no sunny lines):**  \n   - Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,x=n\\) (or a mixture of vertical and horizontal lines) to cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   - All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n\n3. **Increasing the number of sunny lines:**  \n   - Starting from the base configuration, replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line that passes through a point that was covered *only* by the replaced line.  \n   - Choose the sunny line with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and with an intercept that avoids coincidence with any existing line; this keeps all points covered and the lines distinct.  \n   - Repeating this operation \\(k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n4. **Existence for every \\(k\\):**  \n   - The replacement procedure works for any \\(k\\le n\\); thus every integer in \\([0,n]\\) is attainable.  \n\n5. **Impossibility of other values:**  \n   - The only restriction is \\(k\\le n\\); since all values up to \\(n\\) are realizable, no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.  \n\\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering family consisting only of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{ns}= \\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) are parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis or to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\); from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\le k\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).\n\nHence **every** point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) already satisfies the covering requirement, using exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nWe now show how to increase the number of sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\nSuppose we have a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing \\(s\\) sunny lines, where \\(s<k\\).  \nPick a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_\\ell=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }\\ell\\text{ and on no other line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(U_\\ell=\\varnothing\\) we may simply delete \\(\\ell\\) and insert any sunny line (for instance a line of slope \\(2\\) through the