The diagonal has slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), and the horizontals have slope \\(0\\) (forbidden). Hence **no** line in \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) is sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\). The sentence “contains exactly one sunny line … is not sunny” is contradictory; it is a **justification gap** but does not affect the correctness of the claim that \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) yields \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### Section 3 – Sunny lines that pass through many points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n> “For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define the line  \n> \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_c:\\; y = (c-1)\\,x - \\frac{(c-1)c}{2}+1 .\\)”\n\n#### Claim 1 – Slope and “sunny” status\n> “The slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) is \\(c-1\\). Since \\(c-1\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(c\\ge1\\), we have \\(c-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny.”\n\n**Error:**  \n- For \\(c=1\\) we obtain slope \\(0\\), which **is** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and thus **not sunny**.  \n- For \\(c=0\\) (not allowed) slope would be \\(-1\\).  \nHence the statement “\\(c-1\\neq0\\)” is false for \\(c=1\\). Consequently \\(\\ell_1\\) is **not** sunny, contradicting the claim that *all* \\(\\ell_c\\) are sunny. This is a **Critical Error** because the construction later assumes each \\(\\ell_i\\) contributes one sunny line.\n\n#### Claim 2 – Points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_c\\)\n> “For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le c\\) we compute  \n> \\(y_t = (c-1)t - \\frac{(c-1)c}{2}+1 = t - (c-t).\\)  \n> Hence \\((t,y_t)=(t,t)\\).”\n\n**Error:** The algebraic simplification is incorrect.  \nStarting from the definition,\n\\[\ny_t = (c-1)t - \\frac{(c-1)c}{2}+1.\n\\]\nExpanding,\n\\[\ny_t = (c-1)t - \\frac{c^2-c}{2}+1.\n\\]\nThere is **no** simplification that yields \\(t-(c-t)\\). In fact,\n\\[\nt-(c-t)=2t-c,\n\\]\nwhich is generally **not** equal to the expression above. For example, take \\(c=3, t=2\\):\n\\[\ny_t = 2\\cdot2 - \\frac{2\\cdot3}{2}+1 = 4 -3 +1 =2,\n\\]\nwhereas \\(t-(c-t)=2-(3-2)=1\\). Hence the claim that \\((t,t)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\) for all \\(t\\le c\\) is false. Consequently the statement “\\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly \\(c\\) points of \\(T_n\\)” is also false.\n\nBecause the entire subsequent construction (Section 4) relies on the properties “\\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny” and “\\(\\ell_c\\) passes through the whole column \\(x=c\\) (or at least the diagonal points \\((c,c)\\))”, the failure of these claims **invalidates** the construction.\n\n**Conclusion for Section 3:** The definition of \\(\\ell_c\\) does **not** provide the claimed sunny lines, and the derived properties are mathematically incorrect. This is a **Critical Error** that breaks the logical chain of the proof.\n\n---\n\n### Section 4 – Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nThe author defines\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe subsequent arguments that:\n\n* the lines are distinct,\n* exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered,\n\nall **depend** on the false properties of the \\(\\ell_c\\) lines established in Section 3. Since those properties are incorrect, the reasoning in this section collapses. In particular:\n\n* For \\(c=1\\), \\(\\ell_1\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is **not** sunny, contradicting the claim that the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n* The coverage argument (“if \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\)”) is invalid because \\(\\ell_a\\) does **not** contain the whole column \\(x=a\\); it may contain at most a single lattice point (if any), leaving many points uncovered.\n\nThus the construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) fails. This is a direct consequence of the **Critical Error** identified earlier.\n\n---\n\n### Section 5 – Exhaustion of possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n> “We have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family …”\n\n**Verification:** This conclusion is **unsupported** because the families \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) do **not** satisfy the required properties for \\(k\\ge1\\). Hence the statement is false.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe solution correctly handles the trivial bound and provides a valid construction for \\(k=0\\). However, the core of the problem—showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable—relies on a faulty construction. The definition of the “sunny” lines \\(\\ell_c\\) is mathematically incorrect, leading to false claims about their slopes, sunny status, and the points they cover. Consequently the proof does not establish the existence of families with \\(k>0\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Result:** The solution contains a **Critical Error** that invalidates the main construction; therefore the solution is **incorrect**."
