nt lies on the diagonal) or \\(b\\le n-1\\) (hence it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) also covers \\(T_n\\); all its lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. A sunny line containing a diagonal segment\n\nFor an integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_s:\\; y=(s-1)x-\\frac{(s-1)s}{2}+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_s\\) is \\(s-1\\); because \\(s-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(\\sigma_s\\) is sunny.\n\nThe points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((s,s)\\) satisfy (2), so both belong to \\(\\sigma_s\\).  Since a line passing through two points of the main diagonal of the Young diagram must contain the whole diagonal segment between them, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le s\\}\\subseteq\\sigma_s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\sigma_s\\) contains exactly the \\(s\\) diagonal points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(s,s)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n2\\le s_1<s_2<\\dots <s_k\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(k=0\\) the list is empty.)  \nReplace the vertical lines \\(x=s_1,\\dots ,x=s_k\\) of \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_{s_1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{s_k}\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\bigl\\{\\sigma_{s_1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{s_k}\\bigr\\}\n                 \\cup\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  The slopes of the \\(\\sigma_{s_j}\\) are the distinct numbers \\(s_j-1\\); the remaining lines are vertical, so all \\(n\\) lines are different.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_{s_j}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) 