f \\(d\\neq c_j\\) then \\(d\\) is an integer belonging to the interval (5) but different from all chosen \\(c_\\ell\\). Consequently the vertical line \\(x=b\\) is **not** among the removed vertical lines, i.e. \\(b\\notin\\{1+c_1,\\dots ,1+c_k\\}\\). Hence \\(x=b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn every situation \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\); thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe interval \\([-n+2,n-2]\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) even integers; denote them by  \n\n\\[\ne_1<e_2<\\dots <e_n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,n}= \\{\\rho_{e_1},\\rho_{e_2},\\dots ,\\rho_{e_n}\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) put \\(d=b-a\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(-n+2\\le d\\le n-2\\) and \\(a+b\\) has the same parity as \\(d\\). Since \\(a+b\\) is odd when \\(n\\) is odd and even when \\(n\\) is even, \\(d\\) is necessarily even; therefore \\(d=e_j\\) for some \\(j\\). By definition of \\(\\rho_{e_j}\\) we have \\(b=a+e_j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\rho_{e_j}\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers the whole set \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (1), this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct st