have undefined slope while the \\(\\ell_d\\) have slope \\(1\\), and the \\(\\ell_d\\) have distinct intercepts \\(d\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies either on a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\)) or on one of the \\(\\ell_d\\) (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(n-k+1\\)). Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) satisfies the covering requirement, and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling both conditions of the problem, and we have shown that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
