(s-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=:N .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf a sunny line contained three distinct points of \\(S_n\\), those three points would be collinear with a slope different from the three forbidden slopes, which is impossible because any two points already determine the line.  \nThus a sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in **at most two** points.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nThe three forbidden directions are:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=b\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=a\\);\n* diagonal:   \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nFor a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) the admissible points are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); therefore the line contains \\(n+1-b\\) points.  \nThe maximum of this quantity over all admissible \\(b\\) is \\(n\\) (attained for \\(b=1\\)).  \nThe same bound holds for vertical lines.  \n\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\); its cardinality is \\(\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}<n\\).  \n\nConsequently **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\nk=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad\nt=n-k=\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nThe sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points, while the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(tn\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered,\n\n\\[\n2k+tn\\ge N .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(k=n-t\\) and the value of \\(N\\) from (1):\n\n\\[\n2(n-t)+tn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nAfter rearranging we obtain\n\n\\[\nt(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\) is an integer, (3) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFrom \\(k=n-t\\) and (4),\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration can contain at most the number of sunny lines given on the right‑hand side of (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nt_0:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil ,\\qquad\nk_0:=n-t_0 .\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(t_0\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=t_0 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and are pairwise distinct.  \nThe total number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on them is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{t_0}(n+1-b)=t_0n-\\frac{t_0(t_0-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) with \\(t=t_0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_0n-\\frac{t_0(t_0-1)}2\\ge N-2k_0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence at most \\(2k_0\\) points of \\(S_n\\) remain uncovered after the horizontal lines are placed.  \nPair these remaining points arbitrarily; each pair determines a distinct sunny line (the pair cannot be collinear with any of the already used horizontal lines because those have slope \\(0\\)).  \nThus we obtain a configuration consisting of exactly \\(t_0\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k_0\\) sunny lines, i.e. a configuration with **\\(k_0\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nIf we delete one of the horizontal lines, the points that were on it become uncovered.  \nEach such point can be placed on a new sunny line together with any other still‑uncovered point (or alone, if necessary).  \nConsequently, by removing any number \\(r\\) of the horizontal lines and adding \\(r\\) new sunny lines we obtain a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=k_0+r,\\qquad 0\\le r\\le t_0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k_0+r\\) ranges over all integers from \\(k_0\\) up to \\(k_0+t_0=n\\), and we may also start from the extreme case \\(t=n\\) (all lines horizontal, giving \\(k=0\\)), we conclude that **every integer**\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil\n\\]\n\nis attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\bigg|\\;\n0\\le k\\le n-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil\\right\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rceil ,\n\\]\n\nand every integer between \\(0\\) and this maximum can be realised."
