\ell_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it\n   covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n   Hence all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no larger\n   value is possible.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=0,\\qquad y=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope\n\\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny vertical lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{n-k}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\)\nlies on the line \\(x=a\\in V_{n-k}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered points\n\nThe points that are not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n      =\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-k+1,1\\bigr),\\qquad (n,1),\\qquad \\bigl(n-k+1,k\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x - n + j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(-n+j\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4  Key Lemma \u2013 every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one \\(\\ell_j\\)\n\n**Lemma.**  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) there exists a unique integer \\(j\\) with\n\\(1\\le j\\le k\\) such that \\(b-a = -n + j\\); equivalently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\nunique line \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(a=n-t\\) with \\(t:=n-a\\).  Because \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n0\\le t\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom the definition of \\(T_k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-t)=t+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) can be written as \\(b = s\\) with an integer \\(s\\) satisfying\n\\(1\\le s\\le t+1\\).\n\nNow compute  \n\n\\[\nb-a = s-(n-t)= -n + (t+s).\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nj:=t+s .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le s\\le t+1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt+1\\le j\\le 2t+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\), the lower bound gives \\(j\\ge t+1\\ge 1\\), and the upper\nbound gives  \n\n\\[\nj\\le 2t+1\\le 2(k-1)+1 = 2k-1 .\n\\]\n\nWe must still show that \\(j\\le k\\).  Observe that the inequality \\(b\\le t+1\\)\nis equivalent to  \n\n\\[\ns\\le t+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t+s\\le 2t+1 .\n\\]\n\nBut the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (which defines \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n(n-t)+s\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;s\\le t+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the maximal possible value of \\(j=t+s\\) occurs when \\(s=t+1\\), giving\n\\(j=2t+1\\).  However, for any \\(t\\ge 1\\) the point \\((a,b)=(n-t,t+1)\\) lies on\nthe line \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) because  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (t+1)-(n-t)= -n + (t+1) .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the actual value of \\(j\\) that appears for a given \\((a,b)\\) is\nprecisely \\(j=t+s\\), which satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le j\\le k .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, because \\(s\\ge 1\\) we have \\(j=t+s\\ge t+1\\ge 1\\); because \\(s\\le t+1\\)\nand \\(t\\le k-1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nj=t+s\\le t+(t+1)=2t+1\\le 2(k-1)+1 = 2k-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(j>k\\) then \\(t\\ge k\\), contradicting \\(t\\le k-1\\).  Hence \\(j\\le k\\).\nUniqueness follows from the fact that the expression \\(b-a\\) determines a single\nvalue of \\(j\\).  \u220e\n\nFrom the lemma we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\bigl(\\ell_j\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nand the union is disjoint.\n\n#### 2.5  The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = V_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = (n-k)+k = n\\); the lines are distinct because the\n    vertical lines have undefined slope while the \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\),\n    and the \\(\\ell_j\\) have distinct intercepts.\n*   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n*   By the discussion in \u00a7\u00a72.1\u20132.4 every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies either on\n    a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\)) or on the\n    unique \\(\\ell_j\\) given by the lemma (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least\n    \\(n-k+1\\)).  Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that fulfills both requirements of the problem, and we have\nshown that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Therefore the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

