g \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x - n + j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(-n+j\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4  Key Lemma \u2013 the set of differences in \\(T_k\\)\n\n**Lemma.**  \n\\[\n\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\,\\}= \\{-n+1,\\,-n+2,\\dots ,\\,-n+k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a point of \\(T_k\\) as \\((a,b)=(n-t,\\;s)\\) where  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad s:=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we have \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\); because \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\)\nwe have \\(1\\le s\\le n+1-(n-t)=t+1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nb-a = s-(n-t)= -n+(t+s).\n\\]\n\nThe integer \\(t+s\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t+s\\le (k-1)+1 = k,\n\\]\n\nthe lower bound coming from \\(s\\ge1\\) and the upper bound from \\(s\\le t+1\\).\nThus \\(b-a\\) ranges over the \\(k\\) consecutive integers \\(-n+1,\\dots ,-n+k\\),\nand every integer in this range occurs (choose any \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and\ntake \\(t=j-1,\\;s=1\\)).  \u220e\n\nBecause each line \\(\\ell_j\\) consists of all points with \\(b-a=-n+j\\), the Lemma\nimplies that **every** point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n#### 2.5  The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = V_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = (n-k)+k = n\\); the lines are distinct because the vertical\n    lines have undefined slope while the \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\), and the\n    \\(\\ell_j\\) have distinct intercepts.\n*   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n*   By the discussion in \u00a7\u00a72.1\u20132.4 every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies either on\n    a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\)) or on the\n    unique \\(\\ell_j\\) given by the Lemma (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least\n    \\(n-k+1\\)).  Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that fulfills both requirements of the problem, and we have\nshown that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Therefore the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

