------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=0,\\qquad y=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other slopes give sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny vertical lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{n-k}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n\\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in V_{n-k}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered points\n\nThe points that are not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n      =\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-k+1,1\\bigr),\\qquad (n,1),\\qquad \\bigl(n-k+1,k\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x - n + j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(-n+j\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4  Key Lemma \u2013 the set of differences in \\(T_k\\)\n\n**Lemma.**  \n\\[\n\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\,\\}= \\{-n+1,\\,-n+2,\\dots ,\\,-n+k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a point of \\(T_k\\) as \\((a,b)=(n-t,\\;s)\\) where  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad s:=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we have \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\); because \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\)\nwe have \\(1\\le s\\le n+1-(n-t)=t+1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nb-a = s-(n-t)= -n+(t+s).\n\\]\n\nThe integer \\(t+s\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t+s\\le (k-1)+1 = k,\n\\]\n\nthe lower bound coming from \\(s\\ge1\\) and the upper bound from \\(s\\le t+1\\).\nThus \\(b-a\\) ranges over the \\(k\\) consecutive integers \\(-n+1,\\dots ,-n+k\\),\nand every integer in this range occurs (choose any \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and\ntake \\(t=j-1,\\;s=1\\)).  \u220e\n\nBecause each line \\(\\ell_j\\) consists of all points with \\(b-a=-n+j\\), the Lemma\nimplies that **every** point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n#### 2.5  The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = V_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = (n-k)+k = n\\); the lines are distinct because the vertical\n    lines have undefined slope while the \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\), and the\n    \\(\\ell_j\\) have distinct intercepts.\n*   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n*   **Coverage verification.**  \n    \u2013 If a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\), then it lies on\n      the vertical line \\(x=a\\in V_{n-k}\\).  \n    \u2013 If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); by the Lemma there exists a\n      unique \\(j\\) with \\(b-a=-n+j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n      \\(\\ell_j\\).  Hence the point lies on that sunny line.  \n    In either case the point is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--