s ,x=n-2k+1\\).  \n   * The remaining lattice points form the triangle  \n\n     \\[\n     T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-2k+2\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   * For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_j:\\; y = x - n + 2j-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     All \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny; they are distinct\n     because their intercepts \\(-n+2j-1\\) are distinct.\n\n   * **Key Lemma.**  \n\n     \\[\n     \\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\,\\}= \\{-n+1,\\,-n+3,\\dots ,\\,-n+2k-1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the set of differences consists of exactly the \\(k\\) odd integers\n     \\(-n+2j-1\\) \\((j=1,\\dots ,k)\\).\n\n     *Proof.*  \n       Write a point of \\(T_k\\) as \\((a,b)=(n-t,\\;s)\\) with\n       \\(0\\le t\\le 2k-2\\) and \\(1\\le s\\le t+1\\).  Then  \n\n       \\[\n       b-a = s-(n-t)= -n+(t+s).\n       \\]\n\n       Because \\(s\\) and \\(t\\) have opposite parity, \\(t+s\\) is odd and ranges\n       over the odd numbers \\(1,3,\\dots ,2k-1\\).  Hence \\(b-a\\) ranges exactly\n       over the claimed set. ∎\n\n   * By the Lemma, each point of \\(T_k\\) satisfies the equation of precisely\n     one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n3. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has \\(a\\le n-2k+1\\) it lies on the\n     vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   * If \\(a\\ge n-2k+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and, by the Lemma, lies on the\n     unique sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) with intercept \\(-n+2j-1\\).  \n\n   Thus every required lattice point is on at least one line of the family.\n\n4. **Counting.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2k+1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((n-2k+1)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\n   sunny.  Hence the construction works for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility for larger \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(k>\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   sunny lines.  Each sunny line can be assumed, without loss of generality,\n   to have slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Projecting the set\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) onto the axis \\(b-a\\) shows that at most \\(2k-1\\) distinct\n   values of \\(b-a\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines of any slopes, while\n   the triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) forces at least \\(2k+1\\) distinct\n   values when \\(k>\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  This contradiction proves that no such\n   configuration exists.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
