=t\\) we have \\(t-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\in\\sigma_{t-1}\\).  Thus the\n\\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_n\\), proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (flexible construction)\n\nLet \\(s\\) be an integer with \\(-1\\le s\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(s\\) (if \\(s=-1\\) we take\n\\(S=\\varnothing\\)).  Construct the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_t\\mid t\\in S\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny diagonals}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{h\\}}_{\\displaystyle h:y=1}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{v_t\\mid t\\notin S\\}}_{\\displaystyle v_t:x=t-1 } .\n\\]\n\nAll vertical lines \\(v_t\\) are distinct; the horizontal line \\(h\\) appears only once.\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F|=|S|+1+(n-s)=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWe must obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so we delete **one** line from\n\\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*If \\(s<n\\):* let \\(t_0\\) be the smallest element of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\)\n(and delete the vertical line \\(v_{t_0}\\)).  \nThe points of \\(L_{t_0}\\) are still covered:\nthe point \\((t_0-1,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h\\), and the point\n\\((1,t_0-1)\\) lies either on the diagonal \\(\\ell_{t_0}\\) (if \\(t_0\\in S\\)) or on the\nhorizontal line \\(h\\) together with the vertical line \\(v_{t_0+1}\\) (which remains\nbecause \\(t_0+1\\notin S\\)).  Thus the covering property is preserved.\n\n*If \\(s=n\\):* then \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists solely of the \\(n\\) diagonal lines; we\ndelete nothing.\n\nAfter the deletion we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Their composition is:\n\n* the \\(s\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) – non‑sunny;\n* the horizontal line \\(h\\) – non‑sunny;\n* the \\(n-s-1\\) remaining vertical lines \\(v_t\\) – each has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is\n  therefore sunny.\n\nConsequently the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk = \\bigl|\\{v_t\\}\\bigr| = n-s-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) can be any integer from \\(-1\\) to \\(n\\), the corresponding values of\n\\(k\\) run through all integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\):\n\\[\ns=-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k=n,\\qquad\ns=n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k=0,\\qquad\n\\text{and }k=n-s-1\\text{ for the intermediate cases}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible; Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is possible.\nLemma 3 produces, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a covering by\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence every integer\nbetween \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) occurs, and no other value can occur because the number\nof lines is exactly \\(n\\).  ∎\n\nThus the problem is completely solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
