(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) covering \\(S_{n}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\parallel \\mathbf{e}_{x}\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\parallel \\mathbf{e}_{y}\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\parallel \\mathbf{e}_{d}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach of the sets \\(A,B,C\\) can contain **at most one** index.  \nIndeed, if two distinct lines were both horizontal, they would be parallel and distinct, hence they could not intersect.  But any two distinct points of \\(S_{n}\\) with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate must be covered, forcing a horizontal line to contain all points of that row; a second horizontal line would be redundant, contradicting distinctness.  The same argument works for vertical lines and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|A|+|B|+|C|\\le 3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow count the points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on lines of each forbidden direction.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (the longest row is \\(y=1\\) with points \\((1,n),\\dots,(n,1)\\) but only the first \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) of them satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nSimilarly a vertical line contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*  \nSuch a line is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  For a given \\(c\\) the points of \\(S_{n}\\) on it are exactly the pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\).  Hence the number of points on this line equals \\(c-1\\).  Since \\(c\\le n+1\\), the maximum possible is \\(n\\).\n\nIf we had **two or more** sunny lines, then at most one line of each forbidden direction could be used (by (1)), leaving at least \\(n-2\\) lines that must be sunny.  This would force the total number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines to be at most  \n\n\\[\n\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+(n)=2\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+n .\n\\]\n\nBut the set \\(S_{n}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, which for \\(n\\ge 3\\) exceeds the above bound unless **at least two** of the three forbidden directions actually appear among the \\(n\\) lines.  Hence at least two lines are non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Base non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{x}\\colon y=0,\\qquad L_{y}\\colon x=0,\\qquad L_{d}\\colon x+y=0 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   They are respectively parallel to \\(\\mathbf{e}_{x},\\mathbf{e}_{y},\\mathbf{e}_{d}\\).\n\n2. **Diagonal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(4\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{t}\\colon x+y=t-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) satisfies \\(x+y=t-2\\) after translating by \\((-1,-1)\\); consequently each such point lies on \\(D_{t}\\).  The line \\(D_{t}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_{0}= \\{L_{x},L_{y},L_{d}\\}\\cup\\{D_{t}\\mid 4\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\).  In this family the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).\n\n3. **Turning a diagonal into a sunny line.**  \n   For a fixed \\(t\\) (\\(4\\le t\\le n+1\\)) consider the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,t-1),\\qquad P_{2}=(2,t-2).\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   (t-3)x-(t-3)y = (t-3) .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{t}= \\frac{t-2- (t-1)}{2-1}= -1+\\frac{1}{t-3},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any integer \\(t\\ge 4\\).  Hence the line (5) is **sunny**.  Moreover, every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) lies on the same line, because the set \\(\\{(a,b):a+b=t\\}\\) is a translate of the line through \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\).  Thus we may replace \\(D_{t}\\) by the sunny line (5) without losing any required point.\n\n4. **Choosing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Select any subset \\(T\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_{k}= \\{L_{x},L_{y},L_{d}\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{sunny line through }(1,t-1),(2,t-2)\\mid t\\in T\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,D_{t}\\mid t\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n   the three base lines, one line for each integer \\(t\\) between \\(4\\) and \\(n+1\\), and the replacements do not create coincidences because each line is associated with a unique value of \\(t\\).  \n   By construction it contains precisely \\(|T|=k\\) sunny lines, and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on the line corresponding to its sum \\(a+b\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) satisfies both required conditions.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have exhibited admissible families for all such \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
