rrow\\qquad\n   y = -x+\\frac{s-2}{\\,s-3\\,}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(-1+\\dfrac{1}{s-3}\\); for every integer \\(s\\ge 4\\) this slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  Hence the line (5) is **sunny**.  Moreover, because all points of \\(\\Delta _s\\) satisfy \\(a+b=s\\), they all lie on the same line (5); thus (5) also covers the whole diagonal \\(\\Delta _s\\).\n\n   *Choosing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Select any subset \\(T\\subset\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|T|=k\\).  Replace, for each \\(s\\in T\\), the non‑sunny line \\(D_{s}\\) in (4) by the sunny line (5).  Denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\).  Explicitly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{k}= \\{L_{h},L_{v},L_{d}\\}\n   \\cup\\Bigl\\{\\,\\text{sunny line (5) for }s\\in T\\,\\Bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\Bigl\\{\\,D_{s}\\mid s\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus T\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   *Distinctness.*  Each line in \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) is associated with a unique value of \\(s\\) (or with the three special directions), so no two coincide.  \n\n   *Covering property.*  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) let \\(s=a+b\\).  If \\(s\\le 3\\) the point lies on one of the three base lines (2).  If \\(s\\ge 4\\) the point lies on the line assigned to \\(s\\) in (6): either the sunny replacement (5) when \\(s\\in T\\) or the diagonal line (3) otherwise.  Hence every required lattice point is covered.  \n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  The only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) are those coming from the set \\(T\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(|T|=k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Because \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) (the three base lines guarantee at least two non‑sunny lines), the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessity \\(k\\le n-2\\) follows from the requirement of a horizontal and a vertical line (at least two non‑sunny li