|-kn=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-kn,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or slope\n   \\(-1\\)).  Each non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); thus\n   we need  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\ge |T_n|-kn .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (4) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\neq n-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-kn\n   \\iff\n   n^2- kn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-kn\n   \\iff\n   n^2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\iff\n   n\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2},\n   \\]\n   which is always true **except** when the left‑hand side of (4) is\n   strictly smaller than the right‑hand side, i.e. when the two sides are\n   equal and the inequality is forced to be strict because the two families of\n   lines must be *disjoint*.  A direct computation shows that equality can hold\n   only for \\(k=n-1\\); in that case the right‑hand side of (3) equals\n   \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)n=\\frac{n+2}{2}>n\\) (for every \\(n\\ge3\\)), so at least\n   two non‑sunny lines would be required, contradicting the fact that only\n   \\(n-(n-1)=1\\) non‑sunny line is available.  Consequently **\\(k=n-1\\) is\n   impossible**.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-1=i\\,(x-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.\n   Solving \\(y=1+i(x-1)\\) together with the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+1)x\\le n+i\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(x,\\,1+i(x-1)\\bigr)\\mid\n   1\\le x\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Two different slopes cannot meet the integer lattice in two distinct points,\n   so the sets in (8) are pairwise disjoint.  Summing the cardinalities given by\n   (7) over \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which can be verified by a simple induction on \\(n\\).  Hence the \\(n\\)\n   sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Take the first \\(k\\) lines of the family (6), i.e. \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n   By (7) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  A direct inequality shows  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i\\ge n+1-i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(\\frac{n+i}{i+1}=1+\\frac{n-i}{i+1}\\ge 1+\\frac{n-i}{n}\\ge\n   1+\\frac{n-i-1}{1}=n+1-i-1\\), and the floor of a number exceeding\n   \\(n-i-1\\) is at least \\(n+1-i\\).)\n\n   Consequently the sunny lines cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   C(k):=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(n+1-i)\n   =\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2}\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny completion.*  \n   The number of points still uncovered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-C(k)\n   \\le\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side of (12) is exactly \\(|T_{\\,n-k}|\\), i.e. the number of\n   lattice points of the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{\\,n-k}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) that is not covered by the sunny lines satisfies\n   \\(a\\le n-k\\); therefore all uncovered points lie on the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they\n   are distinct from the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (which have non‑zero finite\n   slopes).  Thus we obtain a family of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain every\n   point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the sunny‑line bound (2) the sunny lines can cover at most\n   \\((n-1)n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-(n-1)n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)n=\\frac{n+2}{2}>n\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   points remain uncovered.  A single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\)\n   points, so at least two non‑sunny lines would be required.  This would give\n   at least \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) lines, contradicting the requirement that exactly\n   \\(n\\) lines are used.  Therefore **\\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur**.\n\nCombining the constructions of §§4–5 with the impossibility proved in §6,\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
