-k\\) non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or slope\n   \\(-1\\)).  Each non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n   (the same bound (2) holds for them as well).  Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\ge |T_n|-kn . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|T_n|\\) from (1) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n^2- kn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-kn\n   \\iff\n   n^2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\iff\n   n\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which is true for every \\(n\\ge1\\).  The only way that (4) can fail is when\n   the left‑hand side is **strictly smaller** than the right‑hand side, i.e. when\n   equality in (4) would force the single non‑sunny line to contain more than\n   \\(n\\) points.  Equality in (4) occurs exactly for  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-1,\n   \\]\n\n   because then the right‑hand side of (3) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)n=\\frac{n+2}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n   \\]\n\n   which cannot be covered by the single remaining non‑sunny line (it can hold\n   at most \\(n\\) points).  Therefore **\\(k=n-1\\) is impossible**; all other\n   values of \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary inequality (4).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y= i\\,x-(i-1). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is\n   sunny.  Solving the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y= i\\,x-(i-1),\\\\[2pt]\n   a+b\\le n+1,\\ a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain that the integer points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\bigl(x,\\,i x-(i-1)\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\n   1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of (6).*  \n   From \\(y=i x-(i-1)\\) and the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   x+i x-(i-1)\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(i+1)x\\le n+i,\n   \\]\n\n   which is equivalent to the bound on \\(x\\) displayed in (6).  Since\n   \\(x\\ge1\\) and the right‑hand side is an integer, the set of admissible\n   integer \\(x\\) is precisely the interval stated.\n\n   The sets \\(P_i\\) are pairwise disjoint: if a point \\((x,y)\\) belonged to\n   both \\(P_i\\) and \\(P_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), we would have\n   \\(y=i x-(i-1)=j x-(j-1)\\), i.e. \\((i-j)x=i-j\\), which forces \\(x=1\\) and then\n   \\(y=1\\).  But \\((1,1)\\) lies on **all** the lines \\(\\ell_i\\); to keep the\n   lines distinct we simply delete the common point \\((1,1)\\) from all but one\n   of the families (for definiteness, keep it in \\(P_1\\)).  Removing a single\n   point does not affect the covering property because \\((1,1)\\) is already\n   covered by the remaining line.\n\n   Summing the cardinalities of the (adjusted) families gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   a standard telescoping identity that can be proved by induction on \\(n\\).\n   Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) together contain every\n   point of \\(T_n\\).  This yields a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny\n   lines.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Take the first \\(k\\) lines of the family (5), i.e. \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n   By (6) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  A direct estimate shows  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i\\ge n+1-i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le k). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of (8).*  \n   Since \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(i+1\\le n-1\\).  Write  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+i}{i+1}=1+\\frac{n-i}{i+1}\\ge\n   1+\\frac{n-i}{n-1}=1+\\frac{n-i-1}{n-1}+ \\frac{1}{n-1}\n   > n+1-i-1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the floor of a number exceeding \\(n-i-1\\) is at least \\(n+1-i\\).\n\n   Consequently the sunny lines cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   C(k):=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(n+1-i)\n   =\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2} \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny completion.*  \n   The number of points still uncovered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-C(k)\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side of (10) equals \\(|T_{\\,n-k}|\\), i.e. the number of\n   lattice points of the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{\\,n-k}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover every point of \\(T_n\\) that is not covered by the sunny lines has\n   its \\(x\\)-coordinate at most \\(n-k\\); indeed, if \\(x\\ge n-k+1\\) then\n   \\(c_{\\,n-k+1}\\ge 1\\) would already have been counted in (7).  Hence all\n   uncovered points lie on the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore they\n   are non‑sunny, and they are distinct from the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (which\n   have non‑zero finite slopes).  Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines to the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines gives exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the bound (2) the sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-1)n\\) points of\n   \\(T_n\\).  Hence at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-(n-1)n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)n=\\frac{n+2}{2}>n\n   \\]\n\n   points remain uncovered.  A single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\)\n   points of \\(T_n\\); therefore at least two non‑sunny lines would be needed,\n   contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.  Thus\n   **\\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur**.\n\nCombining the constructions of §§4–5 with the impossibility proved in §6,\nthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
