\n   |\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every line }\\ell . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A family of sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=i\\,x-(i-1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Solving the three inequalities  \n\n   \\[\n   x\\ge 1,\\qquad y=i x-(i-1)\\ge 1,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the set of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i:=\\Bigl\\{\\bigl(x,\\,i x-(i-1)\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\n   1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The families \\((P_i)_{i\\ge 1}\\) are pairwise disjoint except for the\n   common point \\((1,1)\\); we keep \\((1,1)\\) in \\(P_1\\) and delete it from\n   all other \\(P_i\\).  Deleting a single point does not affect the covering\n   property because \\((1,1)\\) remains covered by \\(\\ell_1\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 1.*  \n   Write \\(n=i q_i+r_i\\) with \\(0\\le r_i\\le i\\) (division algorithm).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i q_i+r_i+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =q_i+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r_i+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =q_i+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(q_i=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n   =\\sum_{j=2}^{n+1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{j}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n) .\n   \\]\n\n   The well‑known identity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{j}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (obtained by counting the pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(ab\\le n\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=2}^{n+1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{j}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the extra \\(n\\) gives (6). ∎  \n\n   By Lemma 1 the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) together contain\n   exactly \\(|T_n|\\) points, i.e. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one\n   of them.  Thus a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Adding vertical (non‑sunny) lines.**  \n   For any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_m:\\; x=m\n   \\]\n\n   is not sunny (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  Such a line contains\n   precisely the points \\((m,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-m\\); there are\n   \\(n+1-m\\le n\\) of them, in accordance with (2).\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Take the first \\(k\\) lines of the family (3), namely\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  By (5) they cover the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}P_i .\n   \\]\n\n   *Uncovered points.*  \n   From Lemma 1 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n\\setminus S_k|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (4) one checks that every point of \\(T_n\\setminus S_k\\) has its\n   abscissa \\(x\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le n-k . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if \\(x\\ge n-k+1\\) then choosing \\(i=n-k+1\\) in (4) gives a\n   permissible integer \\(x\\) and the corresponding point belongs to\n   \\(P_{\\,n-k+1}\\subseteq S_k\\), a contradiction.\n\n   Consequently all points not yet covered lie in the vertical strips\n   \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny completion.*  \n   Introduce the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   v_1,\\;v_2,\\dots ,v_{\\,n-k},\n   \\qquad v_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(v_j\\) is non‑sunny, distinct from the sunny lines (which have\n   non‑zero finite slopes), and together they contain every point whose\n   abscissa is \\(j\\).  Hence the union of the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_{n-k}\\)\n   covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny.  This construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\), a\n   concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering\n   condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No further\n   restriction on \\(k\\) exists, because the bound (2) shows that any\n   configuration can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, and the case\n   \\(k=n\\) is already realized.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
