(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,x-\\frac{t}{t-1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.\nDifferent values of \\(t\\) give different slopes, so the lines are distinct.\n\nWe prove that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a=1\\).  \nThen \\(b\\le n\\) and the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_v\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b=1\\).  \nAnalogously, \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) and \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nt:=a+b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  Substituting \\(t\\) into (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,a-\\frac{t}{t-1}\n   =\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b-2}\\,a-\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b-2}\n   =b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) and therefore belongs to \\(L_t\\).\n\nAll three cases cover the whole set \\(T_n\\); thus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_h,L_v,L_2,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every required point and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations with an arbitrary admissible number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k-2\\qquad(\\text{so }r\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nStarting from the configuration of Section\u202f2, replace the sunny lines\n\\(L_3,L_4,\\dots ,L_{r+2}\\) by the following \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines:\n\n\\[\nD_j:\\;x+y=j+2\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,r). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\;x=1,\\;D_1,\\dots ,D_r,\\;\n        L_{r+3},L_{r+4},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThere are \\(2\\) (horizontal and vertical) plus \\(r\\) diagonals plus\n\\((n-2)-r\\) remaining sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nOnly the lines \\(L_{r+3},L_{r+4},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are sunny; their count is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)-r = k .\n\\]\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n1. If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(x=1\\);  \n   if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\n2. If \\(a+b=j+2\\) with \\(3\\le j+2\\le r+2\\) (i.e. \\(1\\le j\\le r\\)), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\n   diagonal \\(D_j\\).\n\n3. Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\ge r+3\\).  \n   Set \\(t:=a+b-1\\); then \\(r+3\\le t\\le n-1\\), so \\(L_t\\) is one of the remaining sunny lines.\n   As in Section\u202f2, substituting this \\(t\\) into (2) shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation\n   of \\(L_t\\); hence the point lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and, for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.  Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

