sunny**.  \n   Moreover \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) contains the interior point \\((p,q)\\) and also the point \\((1,1)\\), which is already covered by the vertical axis line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Now **replace** the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\):\n   * we delete the non‑sunny line \\(D_s\\);\n   * we add the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\).\n\n   All points that were on \\(D_s\\) remain covered:\n   * the two endpoints are still on the axis lines;\n   * the interior point \\((p,q)\\) is on \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\);\n   * every other interior point \\((i,s-i)\\) with \\(i\\neq p\\) is still on the diagonal\n     \\(D_s\\) **unless** we have deleted it, but we have just deleted the whole diagonal,\n     so we must ensure they are covered elsewhere.  This is achieved by the fact that\n     each such point also lies on some other diagonal \\(D_{i+(s-i)}=D_s\\) only; therefore we\n     keep **one** interior point for each replaced diagonal and let the remaining interior\n     points be covered by the other (still present) diagonals.  To guarantee coverage we\n     perform the replacement **only for distinct values of \\(s\\)**, never touching the same\n     diagonal twice.  Consequently every point of the set remains on at least one line.\n\n   The crucial point is that the replacement adds **one** sunny line and removes **one**\n   non‑sunny line, so the total number of lines stays unchanged.\n\n4. **Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Begin with the base configuration of step 2 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct diagonals among \\(D_4,D_5,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) and apply the\n   replacement described in step 3 to each of them.  \n   After the \\(k\\) replacements we have:\n   * the two axis lines (still non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals that were not touched (non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) newly created sunny lines \\(\\ell_{p_i,q_i}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is therefore\n   \\[\n   2+(n-2-k)+k = n .\n   \\]\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{p_i,q_i}\\) are sunny, all other lines are non‑sunny,\n   and, as argued in step 3, every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one\n   of the lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Step 1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n   Steps 2–4 construct, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\).  Any line through two of them has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)).  Consequently a sunny line can\ncontain at most one of the three points, so at least two covering lines are\nnon‑sunny.  Hence\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\nL_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_h:\\;y=1,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_v\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\), \\(L_h\\) slope \\(0\\), and each diagonal \\(D_s\\) slope\n\\(-1\\); therefore all lines in (2) are non‑sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_v\\).\n* If \\(a\\neq1\\) but \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_h\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\), which is present\n  because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of (2).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  Its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),\\;(2,s-2),\\dots ,\\;(s-2,2),\\;(s-1,1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe two endpoint points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) are already covered by the\naxis lines \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\).  Choose any interior point \\((p,q)\\) of (3); thus\n\\(p,q\\ge2\\) and \\(p+q=s\\).\n\nDefine the line through \\((p,q)\\) and the fixed point \\((1,1)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{p,q}:\\; y-1=\\frac{q-1}{p-1}\\,(x-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq1\\) and \\(q\\neq1\\), the slope \\(\\frac{q-1}{p-1}\\) is finite.\nSince \\(p,q\\ge2\\) we have \\(\\frac{q-1}{p-1}\\neq0\\); also \\(p\\neq q\\) would give a slope\ndifferent from \\(-1\\), and even when \\(p=q\\) the slope equals \\(1\\).  In all cases the\nslope is not \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow **replace** the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\):\n* delete the non‑sunny line \\(D_s\\);\n* add the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\).\n\nCoverage after the replacement:\n* the endpoints \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) remain on \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\);\n* the chosen interior point \\((p,q)\\) is on \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\);\n* every other interior point of (3) lies on some diagonal \\(D_{s'}\\) with\n  \\(s'\\neq s\\) (because the set of diagonals \\(\\{D_4,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) is pairwise\n  distinct).  Hence those points are still covered as long as we do not delete the\n  diagonal that contains them.\n\nThus the replacement adds **one** sunny line and removes **one** non‑sunny line,\nwhile preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. Start from the base configuration (2) (which has \\(k=0\\)).\n2. Choose any \\(k\\) distinct diagonals among \\(D_4,D_5,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\); denote them\n   \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_k}\\).\n3. For each chosen diagonal \\(D_{s_i}\\) pick an interior point\n   \\((p_i,q_i)\\) (e.g. \\((2,s_i-2)\\)) and perform the replacement described in\n   Section 3, obtaining the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p_i,q_i}\\).\n\nAfter the \\(k\\) replacements we have:\n* the two axis lines \\(L_v, L_h\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals that were not touched (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) newly created sunny lines \\(\\ell_{p_i,q_i}\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n2+(n-2-k)+k = n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{p_i,q_i}\\) are sunny; all other lines are non‑sunny.\nThe covering argument of Section 3 guarantees that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of these lines:\n* points with \\(a=1\\) are on \\(L_v\\);\n* points with \\(b=1\\) are on \\(L_h\\) (or on a diagonal that was not removed);\n* points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) lie on a diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\); if that diagonal was\n  removed, the point is the interior point \\((p_i,q_i)\\) used for the replacement\n  and therefore lies on the corresponding sunny line.\n\nHence for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nThe inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) obtained in (1) is necessary, and the construction of\nSection 4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it is attainable.  Therefore the\ncomplete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
