q}:\\;y-1=\\frac{q-1}{p-1}\\,(x-1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(p,q\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(\\frac{q-1}{p-1}\\) is finite, non\u2011zero and\n   different from \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   Now **replace** the diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) **and**\n   *add* the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \n   The new line \\(x=p\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\) (non\u2011sunny) and passes through all\n   points of the form \\((p,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\). In particular it contains every\n   interior point of (2) **except** the chosen \\((p,q)\\); those points are\n   \\((p,s-p)\\) with \\(s-p\\neq q\\).  \n\n   Consequently, after the replacement we have:\n\n   * the two original axis lines \\(L_{v},L_{h}\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * the vertical line \\(x=p\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * the sunny line \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\);\n   * all the remaining diagonals \\(D_{t}\\) with \\(t\\neq s\\).\n\n   Every point of the set \\(T_{n}\\) is still covered:\n   * points on \\(L_{v}\\) or \\(L_{h}\\) are unchanged;\n   * points on a diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) with \\(t\\neq s\\) are unchanged;\n   * points of the former diagonal \\(D_{s}\\):\n        - the two endpoints are on \\(L_{v}\\) or \\(L_{h}\\);\n        - the interior point \\((p,q)\\) is on \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\);\n        - every other interior point \\((p,b)\\) (with \\(b\\neq q\\)) lies on the new\n          vertical line \\(x=p\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is unchanged (one diagonal removed, one sunny and\n   one vertical line added, i.e. net change \\(0\\)).\n\n4. **Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Start from the base family of step\u202f2 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \n   For each of \\(k\\) distinct values \\(s\\in\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) perform the\n   replacement described in step\u202f3, using a different interior point for each\n   diagonal.  \n   After the \\(k\\) replacements we have:\n\n   * the two original axis lines \\(L_{v},L_{h}\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * for each replaced diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) a pair consisting of one sunny line\n     \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) and one new vertical line \\(x=p\\) (the latter is non\u2011sunny);\n   * the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals (non\u2011sunny).\n\n   Counting the lines:\n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{axes})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{unchanged diagonals})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\n   \\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{new vertical})\\;=\\,n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{p,q}\\) are sunny, all others are non\u2011sunny,\n   and the covering property holds by the discussion in step\u202f3.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny\n   lines.  Steps\u202f2\u20134 construct, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n   a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

