unny lines.  \n   – The construction above shows that each integer between these two extremes is attainable; therefore the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\nThus the answer is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_{n}\\) is \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\); we must cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a}\\).  \nAll the lines \\(L_{i}\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that replace vertical ones\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_{a}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\subset S_{n}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(C_{a}\\) are collinear: they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}:\\; y=-(a-1)x+(n+1-a). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (1).*  \nTake the two extreme points of \\(C_{a}\\): \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  \nThe slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-a)-1}{a-a}= \\frac{n-a}{0} = -\\,(a-1),\n\\]\n\nand the line with this slope passing through \\((a,1)\\) has equation (1).  \nEvery intermediate point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (1) because substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=n+1-a\\), and the set \\(C_{a}\\) consists precisely of the integer points on the vertical segment between \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{a}\\) equals \\(-\\,(a-1)\\).  \nFor \\(a\\ge 2\\) this slope