s\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the sums \\(s_i\\) are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) numbers are removed from a set of size \\(n\\); consequently \\(|D|=n-k\\).\n\nFor each \\(d\\in D\\) keep the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;x+y=d .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence are not sunny. They are distinct because the values of \\(d\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 3.3 The whole family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\\cup\\{L_d\\mid d\\in D\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) (the \\(M_i\\)) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(M_i\\) by construction (equation (2)).\n\n*Case 2.* \\((a,b)\\neq P_i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen its sum \\(a+b\\) is not among the removed sums \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\); otherwise \\((a,b)\\) would coincide with the corresponding \\(P_i\\). Hence \\(a+b\\in D\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{a+b}\\) (equation (3)).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nThe total number of lines in any admissible configuration is \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). The construction above works for \\(k=n\\) (choose the \\(n\\) points from the lemma and take only the sunny lines \\(M_i\\)), showing that the bound is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines