ost one point to each diagonal.  Consequently the total number of points that the whole family \\(\\mathcal C\\) can provide to a fixed diagonal \\(D_d\\) is at most\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal S|+|\\mathcal N| = n .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(|D_d|=d-1\\le n\\) for every \\(d\\), this bound does not by itself give a contradiction.  The crucial observation is that **to cover all points of a diagonal \\(D_d\\) we need exactly \\(d-1\\) distinct lines that meet \\(D_d\\)**, because each line contributes at most one point (Lemma 1 and Lemma 2).  Hence for each \\(d\\) the family \\(\\mathcal C\\) must contain at least \\(d-1\\) distinct lines intersecting \\(D_d\\).\n\n   Summing these lower bounds over all diagonals we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d=2}^{n+1}(d-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   On the other hand each line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) can intersect at most one point of each diagonal, i.e. it can be counted at most once for each diagonal.  Therefore the total number of “line‑diagonal incidences’’ contributed by the whole family is at most\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n   \\]\n   The inequality\n   \\[\n   n^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\]\n   holds for all \\(n\\ge 1\\); thus the counting does not yet exclude sunny lines.\n\n   The decisive step is to notice that **the only way a diagonal can be intersected by more than one point of a line is if that line itself is the diagonal**.  Indeed, a line different from \\(x+y=d\\) has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny) or a slope different from these (sunny); in either case Lemma 1 or Lemma 2 guarantees at most one intersection with \\(D_d\\).  Consequently, for a diagonal \\(D_d\\) with \\(d\\ge 3\\) (i.e. containing at least two points) the only line that can contribute **more than one** of its points is the diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) itself.\n\n   Suppose \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains a sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\).  Then at least one diagonal, say \\(D_{d_0}\\), is **not** represented by its own diagonal line in \\(\\mathcal C\\) (otherwise we would already have \\(n\\) distinct diagonals and no room for a sunny line).  For this diagonal we must cover its \\(|D_{d_0}|=d_0-1\\ge 2\\) points using only lines that intersect it in at most one point each (Lemma 1).  Hence we need at least \\(d_0-1\\ge 2\\) distinct lines different from the diagonal \\(x+y=d_0\\) to cover \\(D_{d_0}\\).\n\n   But the total number of lines available besides the \\(n-1\\) diagonals that are present is exactly \\(k\\) (the sunny lines).  Therefore we would need \\(k\\ge d_0-1\\ge 2\\).  Repeating the argument for every diagonal that is omitted forces\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\max_{d\\text{ omitted}}(d-1).\n   \\]\n   Since at least one diagonal is omitted, the right‑hand side is at least \\(2\\).  Consequently \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n   However, if \\(k\\ge 2\\) then the family \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) diagonals.  The total number of points contributed by those diagonals is\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d\\in I}(d-1),\n   \\]\n   where \\(I\\) is a set of \\(n-2\\) integers from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  The largest possible sum is obtained by taking the \\(n-2\\) largest integers, i.e. \\(3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\); this sum equals\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2-3 = \\frac{n^{2}+3n-4}{2}.\n   \\]\n   The remaining points (those belonging to the two omitted diagonals) are at least\n   \\[\n   (2-1)+(3-1)=3 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the total number of points that must be supplied by the sunny lines is at least \\(3\\).  Each sunny line can supply at most one point to each of the two omitted diagonals, i.e. at most two points altogether.  With only \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines we can supply at most \\(2k\\) points, which is insufficient when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  This contradiction shows that **no sunny line can be present**.\n\n   Therefore \\(k\\) must be zero.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction with the \\(n\\) diagonal lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and the argument above proves that any configuration cont