            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of  \n\\((1,0),\\;(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nConsider the two extreme points of \\(T_{n}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nSuppose the line \\(\\ell_{1}\\in\\mathcal L\\) that contains \\(P_{1}\\) were sunny.  \nIts slope cannot be \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{1}\\) meets the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) at a second point\n\\(Q\\neq P_{1}\\).  Since \\(Q\\) also satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), we have \\(Q\\in T_{n}\\).\n\nBecause the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct, \\(Q\\) must lie on a line \\(\\ell\\neq\\ell_{1}\\) of the family.  \nBut then \\(\\ell\\) also passes through \\(P_{1}\\) (otherwise \\(P_{1}\\) would be uncovered), contradicting the assumption that the lines are distinct.  \nHence \\(\\ell_{1}\\) cannot be sunny; it must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n\nThe same reasoning applied to \\(P_{2}\\) shows that the line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) containing \\(P_{2}\\) is also non‑sunny.  \n\nThus any admissible configuration contains at least two non‑sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&: x=n-1,\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&: y=n-1,\\\\\n\\ell_{3}^{(j)}&: x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(2+(n-2-k)=n-k\\) lines, all parallel to the coordinate axes, hence non‑sunny.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{k}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{i}\\neq 0,\\;\\alpha_{i}\\neq\\infty,\\;\\alpha_{i}\\neq -1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}^{(i)}:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_{i}\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{s}^{(i)}\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nD_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\in T_{n},\n\\]\n\nwhich lies on the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nBecause the slopes \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_{s}^{(i)}\\) are distinct; none of them is parallel to \\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\), so they are sunny.  \nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification of the covering property  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). We distinguish three mutually exclusive cases.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-2-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{3}^{(a)}\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(a=n-1\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) the point belongs to \\(\\ell_{2}\\); if \\(a=n-1\\) it belongs to \\(\\ell_{1}\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a\\ge n-1-k\\) and \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only points satisfying these inequalities are the diagonal points  \n\n\\[\nD_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nBy construction each \\(D_{i}\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}^{(i)}\\) given by (2).\n\nThese three cases cover every element of \\(T_{n}\\); consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{3}^{(j)}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2-k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{s}^{(i)}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all required lattice points.  The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
