l (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Counting points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A line that contains many points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFrom (3) and (4) the pigeon\u2011hole principle yields a line \\(\\ell\\) among the \\(n\\) that contains at least  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_n|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Geometry of a line with two points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) then its direction is one of the three forbidden ones.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\) with \\((a_1,b_1)\\neq(a_2,b_2)\\).  \nSet \\(\\Delta a=a_2-a_1\\) and \\(\\Delta b=b_2-b_1\\).  \nBecause both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta a+\\Delta b=(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\le 0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta a\\) and \\(\\Delta b\\) have opposite signs, then \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b\\) would be strictly positive, contradicting (6).  \nThus either \\(\\Delta a=0\\) (vertical line), \\(\\Delta b=0\\) (horizontal line), or \\(\\Delta a=-\\Delta b\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). \u220e\n\nConsequently any line that contains **at least two** points of \\(S_n\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. A lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and (5) the line guaranteed by the pigeon\u2011hole principle contains at least \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf \\(m\\ge 2\\) (which holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)), that line is non\u2011sunny.  \n\nMoreover, two distinct non\u2011sunny lines that are parallel cannot both pass through a common point of \\(S_n\\); otherwise they would coincide, contradicting the distinctness of the lines. Hence each of the \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the special line must be covered by a *different* non\u2011sunny line. Therefore at least \\(m\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny.  \n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le n-m=n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the necessary condition announced in the summary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Sharpness of the bound \u2013 constructions\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (3) and attaining equality in (7).\n\nWrite \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n#### 5.1. Even case \\(n=2t\\)\n\nConsider the following \\(2t\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=t\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\n* Vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=t\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2t}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le 2t+1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le t\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); otherwise \\(a>t\\) and the inequality forces \\(b\\le t\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). Hence (3) holds.\n\nNow replace the \\(t\\) vertical lines by the \\(t\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{2j-1}{2}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_j\\) passes through the point \\((2j-1,\\,j)\\in S_{2t}\\) and through no other point of the original vertical family, so the set of covered points is unchanged. The resulting family consists of \\(t\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_j\\)) and \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the horizontals), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk=t=n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n#### 5.2. Odd case \\(n=2t+1\\)\n\nTake the following \\(2t+1\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=t+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n* Vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=t\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{2t+1}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le 2t+2\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le t\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge t+1\\) and the inequality forces \\(b\\le t+1\\), so the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). Thus (3) is satisfied.\n\nReplace the \\(t\\) vertical lines by the sunny lines (8) with \\(j=1,\\dots ,t\\).  \nAgain the covered set does not change, and we obtain exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\((t+1)+t=n\\) lines in total. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk=t=n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (7) we have the universal upper bound on the number of sunny lines.  \nThe constructions of \u00a75 show that this bound is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nSince we can always delete sunny lines (replacing them by any of the three forbidden directions) without breaking the covering property, every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nis realizable.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\,n-\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\}\\; } .\n\\]"
