al line \\(y=j-1\\)). Each replacement reduces \\(k\\) by one while preserving the covering property, because the new non\u2011sunny line still contains the point that \\(\\ell_j\\) covered. Repeating this operation we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary observations\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of these three directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A sunny line can meet \\(S_n\\) in at most one point\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\).  \nTheir difference vector \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)=(a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) is non\u2011zero.  \nSince both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta a+\\Delta b=(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\le 0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta a\\) and \\(\\Delta b\\) have opposite signs, then \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b>0\\), contradicting (3).  \nHence \\(\\Delta a\\) and \\(\\Delta b\\) have the same sign or one of them is zero.  \n\n* If \\(\\Delta a=0\\) the line is vertical (non\u2011sunny).  \n* If \\(\\Delta b=0\\) the line is horizontal (non\u2011sunny).  \n* If \\(\\Delta a,\\Delta b\\neq0\\) and have the same sign, then \\(\\Delta a+\\Delta b>0\\), again contradicting (3).  \n\nThe only remaining possibility is \\(\\Delta a=-\\Delta b\\), i.e. the line has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), which is also non\u2011sunny.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet the \\(n\\) lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) and let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(S_n\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(s\\) points.\n\nAll remaining points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by the \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(S_n\\) (e.g. the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)). Consequently the covering condition imposes **no lower bound** on the number of non\u2011sunny lines beyond the obvious requirement that at least one non\u2011sunny line be present when \\(s\\ge |S_n|\\).\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), we must have at least two non\u2011sunny lines; otherwise the sunny lines could cover at most \\(n-1\\) points, leaving uncovered points. Hence\n\n\\[\nn-s\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad s\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction achieving \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake the horizontal line \\(\\ell_H: y=1\\) and the vertical line \\(\\ell_V: x=1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{2j-3}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_j\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(2j-3,\\;j-1).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac12(2j-3)+\\frac{2j-3}{2}=j-1,\n\\]\nso \\(P_j\\in\\ell_j\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n(2j-3)+(j-1)=3j-4\\le n+1\\quad\\text{for }j\\le n,\n\\]\nhence \\(P_j\\in S_n\\). Distinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct points, so the \\(\\ell_j\\) are distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) either has first coordinate \\(1\\) (hence lies on \\(\\ell_V\\)), or second coordinate \\(1\\) (hence lies on \\(\\ell_H\\)), or both coordinates at least \\(2\\). The latter points are precisely the set \\(\\{P_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\), each of which lies on the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\). Therefore the union of the two non\u2011sunny lines and the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines, showing that the bound (4) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration above, replace any sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) by a non\u2011sunny line that still passes through its unique point \\(P_j\\) (for instance, replace \\(\\ell_j\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=j-1\\)). This operation reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property. Repeating the replacement we can achieve any integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) holds for every admissible configuration, and the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized. Hence the complete set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\b