f a point was on a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in S\\), then it is either \\((c,1)\\) or \\((c,n+1-c)\\). Both of these points also lie on the replacement line \\(\\tilde\\ell_c\\) (by construction), hence they stay covered.  \nAll other points are on lines that have not been altered, so they are still covered.  \n\n**Distinctness.**  \nFor distinct \\(c_1,c_2\\in S\\) the slopes \\(\\tilde m_{c_1}\\) and \\(\\tilde m_{c_2}\\) are different because the formula \\(\\tilde m_c=\\frac{c-1}{2c-n-1}\\) is injective on the integer interval \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\). Consequently the lines \\(\\tilde\\ell_{c_1}\\) and \\(\\tilde\\ell_{c_2}\\) are distinct.  \nEach \\(\\tilde\\ell_c\\) is not parallel to any of the unchanged lines (vertical, horizontal, or diagonal), because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\). Hence all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are distinct.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequences  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain that for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains an open question within the present analysis. ∎"
