nt, eventually violating the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Therefore a\nsunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous.  \nThe line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points\n\\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\); there are \\(n-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\nThe triangle \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-a)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\)\nand contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most\n\\(2\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points.\nHence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le 2k+n\\,(n-k)=n^2-(n-2)k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\le n^2-(n-2)k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines can appear**.  In particular the cases\n\\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### Base configuration (no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=n-1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to one of them:\n\n* if \\(a\\le n-2\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n* if \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) and the point is \\((n-1,1)\\), which lies on\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* if \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and the point also lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* the point \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Sunny replacement lines\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;\\text{the line through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n-c).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBoth endpoints belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\nc+(1)=c+1\\le n+1,\\qquad (c+1)+(n-c)=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{(n-c)-1}{(c+1)-c}=n-c-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\), we have \\(m_c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\); consequently\n\\(m_c\\neq0\\), \\(m_c\\neq -1\\) and the line is not vertical.  Thus every\n\\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny.  Moreover the map \\(c\\mapsto m_c\\) is injective, so the\nlines \\(\\ell_c\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with any\nline of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n#### Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and choose an arbitrary subset\n\\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).\nConstruct  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{\\,x=c\\mid c\\in S\\,\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_c\\mid c\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines; exactly the lines\n\\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\in S\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf a point of \\(T_n\\) lay on a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\notin S\\), that\nline is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nIf \\(c\\in S\\), the only points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(x=c\\) are\n\\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\); both belong to \\(\\ell_c\\) by definition.\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) lie on lines that have not been altered.\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration\n  of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n  \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
