\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{i}&:x=i\\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\[2mm]\nM&:y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(L_{i}\\) are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(M\\) is horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis); hence none of them is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_{a}\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(M\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThe lines are distinct, so we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines satisfying the required covering property. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Incrementing the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we already have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k<n\\).  \nWe shall construct from \\(\\mathcal F\\) a new family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nWithout loss of generality suppose this line is the vertical line \\(x=c\\) (the other two possibilities are treated analogously).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a=c\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are covered **only** by the line \\(x=c\\) (i.e. they are not on any other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\)).  \nSince the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are distinct, \\(S\\neq\\varnothing\\).\n\nChoose a small angle \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and rotate the line \\(x=c\\) about the point \\((c,1)\\) by \\(\\varepsilon\\) clockwise.  \nDenote the rotated line by  \n\n\\[\nL':\\; y-1 = \\tan(\\varepsilon)\\,(x-c).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\varepsilon\\) is arbitrarily small, \\(L'\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directi