 lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. Hence the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\nH   &: y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(H\\) is horizontal; consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-1},H\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(H\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tool for increasing the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is non‑sunny.  \nAmong the non‑sunny lines choose a **vertical** one; such a line exists because the original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) consists solely of vertical lines and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nDenote this line by  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad\\text{with }1\\le c\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Existence of a private point on \\(V_c\\).*  \nIn the original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) the point \\((c,2)\\) lies on \\(V_c\\) and on no other line (the only other line that meets the column \\(x=c\\) is \\(y=1\\), which does not contain \\((c,2)\\)).  \nWhen we replace vertical lines, we shall always use the **current** private point of a vertical line; after a replacement the new sunny line still contains that point, so the point remains covered but is no longer private.  \nAll vertical lines that have not yet been replaced keep their original private point \\((c,2)\\).  \nConsequently, as long as at least one vertical line has not been replaced, there exists a point of the form \\((c,2)\\) that is covered **only** by that vertical line.\n\n*Replacement.*  \nSelect a vertical line \\(V_c\\) that still possesses its private point \\((c,2)\\).  \nChoose any slope  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n\\]\n\nand define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; y-2=m\\,(x-c).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_c\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.  \nBecause \\((c,2)\\) lies on both \\(V_c\\) and \\(L_c\\), that point stays covered after the replacement.\n\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(V_c\\) have coordinates \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 3\\).  \nSuch a point is **not** on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and, by construction, is not on any other vertical line (different vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates).  \nTherefore after removing \\(V_c\\) these points would become uncovered **unless** they are already covered by some other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nIn the original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) the only line that could also contain \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 3\\) is a vertical line, which does not exist for that \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nHence the only way \\((c,b)\\) remains covered after the removal of \\(V_c\\) is that it is already covered by a line different from \\(V_c\\) in the current family \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nWhen we perform the replacement we do **not** delete any of those other covering lines, so the point stays covered.\n\nConsequently the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_c\\})\\cup\\{L_c\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and now has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the former \\(k\\) sunny lines together with \\(L_c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Inductive construction of families with any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \nApply the replacement described in §2 repeatedly:\n\n* after the first replacement we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_1\\) with exactly one sunny line;\n* after the second replacement we obtain \\(\\mathcal F_2\\) with exactly two sunny lines;\n* …\n* after the \\(k\\)-th replacement we obtain \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nAt each step we are able to choose a vertical line that still possesses a private point \\((c,2)\\) because we have replaced at most \\(k-1\\) vertical lines, leaving at least one untouched vertical line from the original set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\}\\).  \nThus the procedure can be carried out for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Exhaustion of possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, a family contains precisely \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than \\(n\\) nor negative.  \nHence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
