t lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is non‑sunny.  \nAmong the non‑sunny lines choose a **vertical** one; such a line exists because the original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) consists solely of vertical lines and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nDenote this line by  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad\\text{with }1\\le c\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Existence of a private point on \\(V_c\\).*  \nIn the original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) the point \\((c,2)\\) lies on \\(V_c\\) and on no other line (the only other line that meets the column \\(x=c\\) is \\(y=1\\), which does not contain \\((c,2)\\)).  \nWhen we replace vertical lines, we shall always use the **current** private point of a vertical line; after a replacement the new sunny line still contains that point, so the point remains covered but is no longer private.  \nAll vertical lines that have not yet been replaced keep their original private point \\((c,2)\\).  \nConsequently, as long as at least one vertical line has not been replaced, there exists a point of the form \\((c,2)\\) that is covered **only** by that vertical line.\n\n*Replacement.*  \nSelect a vertical line \\(V_c\\) that still possesses its private point \\((c,2)\\).  \nChoose any slope  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n\\]\n\nand define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; y-2=m\\,(x-c).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_c\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.  \nBecause \\((c,2)\\) lies on both \\(V_c\\) and \\(L_c\\), that point stays covered after the replacement.\n\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(V_c\\) have coordinates \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 3\\).  \nSuch a point is **not** on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and, by construction, is not on any other vertical line (different vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates).  \nTherefore after removing \\(V_c\