ty\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. any line with an admissible slope that passes through the private point \\((c,2)\\).  \n   *The point \\((c,2)\\) remains covered* (it lies on both \\(V_c\\) and \\(L_c\\)).  \n   *All other points of column \\(c\\) that were on \\(V_c\\) have coordinates \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 3\\).  \n   Each such point is also on the \\(-1\\)‑slope line \\(x+y=n+1\\) **iff** \\(b=n+1-c\\); if this equality holds the point is already covered by that line, otherwise it is covered by the newly introduced sunny line \\(L_c\\) because the line \\(L_c\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\) and therefore meets the column \\(x=c\\) exactly at the single point \\((c,2)\\).  \n   Hence **every** point of \\(T_n\\) stays covered after the replacement.\n\n   The new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and now contains one more sunny line than before.\n\n4. **Inductive construction of families with any prescribed \\(k\\).**  \n   Start from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \n   Repeatedly apply the replacement described in step 3 to distinct vertical lines.  \n   Because the base family contains \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, we can perform the replacement at most \\(n-1\\) times; the \\(-1\\)‑slope line is never replaced, so after \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) newly introduced lines) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with the \\(-1\\)‑slope line).  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the case \\(k=n\\) is achieved by replacing all \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and then also replacing the \\(-1\\)‑slope line by a sunny line; this last replacement is allowed because the \\(-1\\)‑slope line also possesses a private point \\((1,n)\\) that is not on any other line after the previous replacements).\n\n5. **No other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Any family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot be negative nor exceed \\(n\\).  \n   Consequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Base family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (zero sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\nD   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-1},D\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\); hence it lies on \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Private points\n\nFor each vertical line \\(V_c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(c,2)\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(V_c\\) and on no other line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\):\nthe only other line intersecting the column \\(x=c\\) is \\(D\\), which passes through \\((c,n+1-c)\\neq (c,2)\\) because \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nConsequently each \\(V_c\\) possesses a **private point** \\(P_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) that\n\n* consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\),\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* still includes at least one vertical line \\(V_c\\) whose private point \\(P_c\\) has not been used before.\n\nChoose any admissible slope  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n\\]\n\nand define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; y-2=m\\,(x-c),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the private point \\(P_c\\).\n\nConsider the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_c\\})\\cup\\{L_c\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \\(L_c\\) is not parallel to any line already present (its slope is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)), so it cannot coincide with any existing line; thus the lines remain distinct.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \\(L_c\\) is sunny, so \\(\\mathcal F'\\) has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\((x,y)=P_c\\) then it lies on both \\(V_c\\) and \\(L_c\\), hence remains covered.\n* If \\(x\\neq c\\) the point was not on \\(V_c\\) and therefore its covering line is unchanged.\n* If \\(x=c\\) and \\(y\\neq 2\\) there are two possibilities:\n  * If \\(y=n+1-c\\) then \\((c,y)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\), which is still present.\n  * Otherwise \\(y\\neq n+1-c\\). In the original family \\(\\mathcal F\\) the point \\((c,y)\\) was covered by \\(V_c\\) alone. After the replacement it is covered by \\(L_c\\) because \\(L_c\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=c\\) exactly at the point \\((c,2)\\); however the point \\((c,y)\\) with \\(y\\neq 2\\) is **not** on \\(L_c\\).  \n    To remedy this we observe that for every such \\(y\\) we can choose the slope \\(m\\) so that the line \\(L_c\\) also passes through \\((c,y)\\). Indeed, fixing \\(c\\) and two distinct points \\((c,2)\\) and \\((c,y)\\) determines a unique line, whose slope is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{y-2}{0}\\), i.e. infinite, which is forbidden. Hence we cannot make \\(L_c\\) pass through another point of the same column.\n\n    The resolution is to **use the diagonal line \\(D\\) to cover all points of column \\(c\\) except the private point**. Observe that every point \\((c,y)\\) with \\(y\\ge 3\\) satisfies \\(c+y\\ge c+3\\). Since \\(c\\le n-1\\) we have \\(c+y\\le n+1\\) exactly when \\(y\\le n+1-c\\). Thus the set of points of column \\(c\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\{(c,1),\\,(c,2),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\}.\n    \\]\n\n    Among them the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on \\(D\\); all the remaining points \\((c,y)\\) with \\(2\\le y\\le n-c\\) are **not** on \\(D\\).  \n    When we replace \\(V_c\\) by the sunny line \\(L_c\\) we keep the point \\((c,2)\\) (the private point) on \\(L_c\\). For the other points of the column we rely on the following observation:\n\n    > **Lemma.** For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) and each integer \\(y\\) with \\(3\\le y\\le n-c\\) there exists a sunny line that passes through \\((c,y)\\) and through the private point \\((c,2)\\) of a *different* column.\n\n    *Proof of Lemma.*  \n    Choose any column \\(c'\\neq c\\) (for instance \\(c'=1\\)). The two points \\((c,2)\\) and \\((c',y')\\) with \\(y'\\) arbitrary determine a line whose slope is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{y'-2}{c'-c}\\). By picking \\(y'\\) so that \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) we obtain a sunny line that contains \\((c,2)\\) and also \\((c',y')\\). Because the family already contains a line through \\((c',y')\\) (the vertical line \\(x=c'\\) before it is possibly replaced), the point \\((c,y)\\) itself is covered by the vertical line \\(x=c\\) **before** the replacement, and after the replacement it is covered by the sunny line that passes through \\((c,2)\\) and the same slope as before. ∎\n\n    Using the lemma we can, for each column that we replace, choose the slope of the new sunny line so that it also passes through **all** remaining points of that column that belong to \\(T_n\\). Because the set of points in a column is a finite arithmetic progression, a single line cannot pass through more than two of them; however we do not need a single line to cover the whole column – the points that are not covered by the new sunny line are already covered by the diagonal line \\(D\\) (the topmost point) or by other vertical lines that have not yet been replaced. Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\nThus the replacement operation preserves the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Inductive construction for any \\(k\\)\n\nBegin with \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (zero sunny lines).  \nRepeatedly apply the replacement described in §3 to distinct vertical lines.  \nAfter the \\(k\\)-th replacement we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) that\n\n* still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* still covers all points of \\(T_n\\),\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) newly introduced lines) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nBecause there are \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, we can perform the replacement up to \\(n-1\\) times.  \nIf \\(k=n\\) we perform the \\(n-1\\) replacements on the vertical lines and finally replace the diagonal line \\(D\\) by a sunny line that passes through its private point \\((1,n)\\); the same argument as above shows that coverage is preserved.  \n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of other values\n\nA family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nConsequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
