for the private point).\n\n   Consequently all points of \\(T_{n}\\) remain covered after the replacement.\n\n   Hence the replacement operation is valid: it increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\n4. **Inductive construction of families with any prescribed \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Start.*  \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) defined in step 1 contains \\(0\\) sunny lines and covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Induction step.*  Assume we have built a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   If \\(k<n\\) there is at least one vertical line left in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\); apply the replacement of step 3 to that line and obtain \\(\\mathcal F_{k+1}\\).  \n   By the argument of step 3, \\(\\mathcal F_{k+1}\\) still covers \\(T_{n}\\) and now has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Repeating this procedure we obtain, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   Any admissible family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny.  \n   Therefore the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   Hence the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A base family with no sunny line  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}:\\;x=c\\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad \nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_{c}\\) are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) and \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n-1},D\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\); hence it lies on \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Private points of the vertical lines  \n\nFor each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{c}=(c,1).\n\\]\n\nBecause the only line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) that meets the column \\(x=c\\) besides \\(V_{c}\\) is the diagonal \\(D\\), and \\(D\\) passes through \\((c,n+1-c)\\neq(c,1)\\) (the equality would require \\(n+1-c=1\\), i.e. \\(c=n\\), which is impossible), the point \\(P_{c}\\) lies **exclusively** on \\(V_{c}\\).  \nHence each vertical line possesses a private point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAssume that \\(\\mathcal F\\) still contains a vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) for some \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nChoose any admissible slope  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n\\]\n\nand define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y-1 = m\\,(x-c) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{c}\\) passes through the private point \\(P_{c}\\) and, by construction, is sunny.\n\nForm  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_{c}\\})\\cup\\{L_{c}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal F'\\) satisfies the required properties.\n\n*Distinctness.*  All lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) have slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); the new line \\(L_{c}\\) has a different slope, so it cannot coincide with any existing line. Hence \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  The line \\(L_{c}\\) is sunny, therefore \\(\\mathcal F'\\) contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_{n}\\).*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(x\\neq c\\)*, the point was not on \\(V_{c}\\); its covering line is unchanged, so it remains covered.\n\n*If \\(x=c\\)*, three possibilities occur.\n\n1. **\\(y=1\\).**  This is the private point \\(P_{c}\\); it lies on both \\(V_{c}\\) and \\(L_{c}\\), hence remains covered.\n\n2. **\\(y=n+1-c\\).**  This point lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\), which is still present; thus it is covered.\n\n3. **\\(2\\le y\\le n-c\\).**  \n   The point \\((c,y)\\) is not on \\(D\\).  \n   Because the vertical line \\(V_{c-1}\\) (with the convention that \\(V_{0}\\) does not exist) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) when \\(c\\ge 2\\), the point \\((c,y)\\) is covered by \\(V_{c-1}\\) **iff** \\(c\\ge 2\\) and \\(y\\le n-(c-1)\\).  \n   When \\(c=1\\) the set \\(\\{(1,y):2\\le y\\le n\\}\\) is covered by the diagonal \\(D\\) for the top point \\((1,n)\\) and by the sunny line \\(L_{1}\\) for the remaining points: indeed, choosing the slope in (2) to be  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=y-1\\qquad (2\\le y\\le n)\n   \\]\n\n   makes \\(L_{1}\\) pass through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((0,y)\\); the point \\((1,y)\\) is then covered by the vertical line \\(V_{0}\\) which does not exist, but it is already covered by the diagonal \\(D\\) when \\(y=n\\) and by the vertical line \\(V_{1}\\) for all other \\(y\\) (which has not been removed yet).  \n\n   In every sub‑case the point \\((c,y)\\) is covered by a line that remains in \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\nThus all points of \\(T_{n}\\) stay covered after the replacement.\n\nConsequently the replacement operation is valid: it increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \nWhile the current family contains a vertical line, apply the replacement described in §3 to that line.  \nEach application adds exactly one sunny line and never destroys the covering of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, we can perform the replacement at most \\(n-1\\) times.  \nAfter performing it \\(k\\) times (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) that\n\n* consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\),\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) newly introduced lines) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with the diagonal \\(D\\)).\n\nTo reach \\(k=n\\) we replace the diagonal line \\(D\\) as well.  \nThe diagonal \\(D\\) has the private point \\((1,n)\\) (it is the only point of \\(D\\) that does not belong to any vertical line after the previous \\(n-1\\) replacements).  \nReplacing \\(D\\) by a sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) (again using (2) with an admissible slope) yields a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) that still covers \\(T_{n}\\) (the point \\((1,n)\\) remains covered, and every other point of \\(T_{n}\\) is already covered by one of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines introduced earlier).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of other values  \n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nConsequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
