dditional sunny lines** and still has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to one of the following three groups:\n\n   * **Group 1.** Points on the column \\(x=c\\).  \n     – The point \\((c,1)=P_{c}\\) lies on \\(L_{c}\\).  \n     – The point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the old diagonal \\(D\\); after the replacement it is covered by the intersection of \\(L_{c}\\) and \\(L_{D}\\) (by construction we forced this intersection to be exactly \\((c,n+1-c)\\)).  \n     – All other points of the column have \\(y\\le n-c\\). Such a point \\((c,y)\\) also lies on the vertical line \\(V_{c-1}\\) (which is still present) because \\(c-1\\ge 1\\) and \\(y\\le n-(c-1)\\). Hence it remains covered.\n\n   * **Group 2.** Points on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) different from \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n     After the replacement the diagonal is no longer present, but every such point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\neq c\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (still present) or on the new sunny line \\(L_{D}\\) (which passes through \\((1,n)\\) and, by the choice of its slope, also through all points of the former diagonal except \\((c,n+1-c)\\)). Hence the whole diagonal remains covered.\n\n   * **Group 3.** All remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\) (those with \\(x\\neq c\\) and not on the diagonal).  \n     Each of them was originally covered by the vertical line \\(V_{x}\\); that line has not been removed, so the point stays covered.\n\n   Consequently the replacement preserves the covering property while increasing the number of sunny lines by **two**.\n\n4. **Inductive construction of families with any prescribed \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Start.*  \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *Induction step.*  Suppose we have built a family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\)).  \n   If \\(t\\le n-2\\) there is at least one vertical line still present; pick any such line \\(V_{c}\\).  \n   Apply the two‑step replacement described in §3 to \\(V_{c}\\) and to the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t+2}\\) has exactly \\(t+2\\) sunny lines and still covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   Repeating this operation we obtain families with sunny‑line numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   0,2,4,\\dots ,2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain the **odd** values of \\(k\\) we perform a single “partial” replacement: replace a vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) by a sunny line \\(L_{c}\\) **and keep the diagonal \\(D\\)**.  \n   The same coverage analysis as in §3 (but without the need to treat the diagonal points) shows that this operation adds exactly one sunny line and preserves the covering property.  \n   Starting from any even‑\\(k\\) family we may therefore reach the next odd number \\(k+1\\).\n\n   Consequently, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   Any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny.  \n   Hence the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   Therefore the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Base family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{c}:\\;x=c\\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad \nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_{c}\\) have slope \\(\\infty\\) and \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n-1},D\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_{a}\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\); hence it lies on \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Private points\n\nFor each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_{c}=(c,1).\n\\]\n\nThe only line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) that meets the column \\(x=c\\) besides \\(V_{c}\\) is the diagonal \\(D\\), which passes through \\((c,n+1-c)\\neq(c,1)\\).  \nHence \\(P_{c}\\) lies **only** on \\(V_{c}\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D\\) has the private point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(1,n),\n\\]\n\nbecause no vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\) contains this point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacement operations  \n\nWe describe two operations that keep the covering property while increasing the number of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1. One‑sunny‑line replacement  \n\nLet \\(V_{c}\\) be a vertical line still present in a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) that covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \nReplace \\(V_{c}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y-1 = m\\,(x-c),\\qquad \nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G'=(\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{V_{c}\\})\\cup\\{L_{c}\\}\n\\]\n\nhas one more sunny line.  \n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) is either on the unchanged vertical line \\(V_{c-1}\\) (if \\(c\\ge 2\\)) or on the diagonal \\(D\\); the only points that could be lost are those that were covered **exclusively** by \\(V_{c}\\). Those are precisely the private point \\(P_{c}\\) and the diagonal point \\((c,n+1-c)\\). Both remain covered: \\(P_{c}\\) lies on \\(L_{c}\\) by construction, and \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\), which has not been removed. Hence \\(\\mathcal G'\\) still covers all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2. Two‑sunny‑line replacement  \n\nLet both a vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) and the diagonal \\(D\\) be present in a covering family \\(\\mathcal H\\).  \nReplace them by the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{c}&:\\; y-1 = m\\,(x-c),\\\\\nL_{D}&:\\; y-n = m'\\,(x-1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad \nm,m'\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nchoosing the slopes so that the two new lines intersect at the lattice point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) (which belongs to \\(T_{n}\\)).  \nExplicitly we may take  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{n+1-c-1}{c-c}= \\text{any admissible value},\\qquad\nm':=\\frac{(c,n+1-c)- (1,n)}{c-1}= \\frac{c-1}{c-1}=1,\n\\]\n\nand then adjust \\(m\\) slightly (still admissible) so that the intersection is exactly \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H'=(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{V_{c},D\\})\\cup\\{L_{c},L_{D}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains **two** additional sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) fall into the three groups described in §3.1; the argument there shows that after the replacement every point remains on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal H'\\). In particular the former diagonal point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) is now covered by the intersection of \\(L_{c}\\) and \\(L_{D}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction of families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nWe build families \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) inductively.\n\n*Base.*  \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) is the family defined in §1; it has \\(0\\) sunny lines and covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) has been constructed, covers \\(T_{n}\\), and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(t\\le n-2\\) there is at least one vertical line still present.  \n  – Apply the **one‑sunny‑line replacement** of §3.1 to any such vertical line.  \n  – The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\) has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(t\\le n-3\\) and we wish to increase the count by two, we may apply the **two‑sunny‑line replacement** of §3.2 to a vertical line together with the diagonal.  \n  – This yields \\(\\mathcal F_{t+2}\\) with \\(t+2\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the one‑step replacement can be performed as long as at least one vertical line remains, we can reach every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nWhen \\(k=n\\) we simply replace **all** vertical lines and the diagonal by sunny lines, using the two‑step replacement repeatedly; after the last replacement no non‑sunny line is left, so the family contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of other values  \n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or not sunny.  \nHence the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nTherefore the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
