q(c,1)\\).  \nHence \\(P_{c}\\) lies **only** on \\(V_{c}\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D\\) has the private point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(1,n),\n\\]\n\nbecause no vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\) contains this point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacement operations  \n\nWe describe two operations that keep the covering property while increasing the number of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1. One‑sunny‑line replacement  \n\nLet \\(V_{c}\\) be a vertical line still present in a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) that covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \nReplace \\(V_{c}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y-1 = m\\,(x-c),\\qquad \nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G'=(\\mathcal G\\setminus\\{V_{c}\\})\\cup\\{L_{c}\\}\n\\]\n\nhas one more sunny line.  \n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) is either on the unchanged vertical line \\(V_{c-1}\\) (if \\(c\\ge 2\\)) or on the diagonal \\(D\\); the only points that could be lost are those that were covered **exclusively** by \\(V_{c}\\). Those are precisely the private point \\(P_{c}\\) and the diagonal point \\((c,n+1-c)\\). Both remain covered: \\(P_{c}\\) lies on \\(L_{c}\\) by construction, and \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\), which has not been removed. Hence \\(\\mathcal G'\\) still covers all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2. Two‑sunny‑line replacement  \n\nLet both a vertical line \\(V_{c}\\) and the diagonal \\(D\\) be present in a covering family \\(\\mathcal H\\).  \nReplace them by the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{c}&:\\; y-1 = m\\,(x-c),\\\\\nL_{D}&:\\; y-n = m'\\,(x-1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad \nm,m'\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nchoosing the slopes so that the two new lines intersect at the lattice point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) (which belongs to \\(T_{n}\\)).  \nExplicitly we may take  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{n+1-c-1}{c-c}= \\text{any admissible value},\\qquad\nm':=\\frac{(c,n+1-c)- (1,n)}{c-1}= \\frac{c-1}{c-1}=1,\n\\]\n\nand then adjust \\(m\\) slightly (still admissible) so that the intersection is exactly \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H'=(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{V_{c},D\\})\\cup\\{L_{c},L_{D}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains **two** additional sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) fall into the three groups described in §3.1; the argument there shows that after the replacement every point remains on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal H'\\). In particular the former diagonal point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) is now covered by the intersection of \\(L_{c}\\) and \\(L_{D}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction of families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nWe build families \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) inductively.\n\n*Base.*  \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) is the family defined in §1; it has \\(0\\) sunny lines and covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) has been constructed, covers \\(T_{n}\\), and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(t\\le n-2\\) there is at least one vertical line still present.  \n  – Apply the **one‑sunny‑line replacement** of §3.1 to any such vertical line.  \n  – The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\) has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(t\\le n-3\\) and we wish to increase the count by two, we may apply the **two‑sunny‑line replacement** of §3.2 to a vertical line together with the diagonal.  \n  – This yields \\(\\mathcal F_{t+2}\\) with \\(t+2\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the one‑step replacement can be performed as long as at least one vertical line remains, we can reach every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nWhen \\(k=n\\) we simply replace **all** vertical lines and the diagonal by sunny lines, using the two‑step replacement repeatedly; after the last replacement no non‑sunny line is l