nes, each of which is either sunny or not sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\). Therefore the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(i,1\\bigr)+t\\bigl(1,\\;n-i-1\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,1),\\qquad Q_{i}=(i+1,\\;n-i).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(m_{i}=n-i-1\\). Because \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nm_{i}\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(m_{i}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nDefine also  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n}:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(n,1\\bigr)+t\\bigl(1-n,\\;n-1\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBefore a tiny rotation \\(\\ell_{n}\\) would have slope \\(-1\\); rotate the whole picture by an irrational angle \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon=\\sqrt2\\) radians). After rotation each line still passes through the same two lattice points, but the slope of \\(\\ell_{n}\\) becomes \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\), which is admissible. Hence \\(\\ell_{n}\\) is also sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n}= \\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\)* then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((a,b)=(n,1)\\in\\ell_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\)* then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n\n- When \\(b=1\\) we have \\((a,b)=P_{a}\\in\\ell_{a}\\).  \n- When \\(b=n+1-a\\) we have \\((a,b)=Q_{a}\\in\\ell_{a}\\).  \n- For any intermediate integer \\(b\\) with \\(1<b<n+1-a\\),\n\n  \\[\n  (a,b)=P_{a}+ \\frac{b-1}{\\,n-a-1\\,}\\,(1,n-a-1),\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\(P_{a}\\) and \\(Q_{a}\\), namely \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacing sunny lines by non‑sunny ones while preserving coverage  \n\nWe now describe three elementary replacements; each replaces one sunny line by a line of a forbidden direction and leaves the covering property intact.\n\n---\n\n**(a) Vertical replacement.**  \nChoose an index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Replace \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage after the replacement.*  \nAll points of the column \\(x=i\\) are of the form \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\).\n\n- The endpoints \\((i,1)=P_{i}\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)=Q_{i}\\) are still covered because they also lie on the neighbouring sunny line \\(\\ell_{i-1}\\) (if \\(i\\ge 2\\)) or on \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (if \\(i\\le n-2\\)); for the extreme cases \\(i=1\\) and \\(i=n-1\\) the points belong to \\(\\ell_{n}\\) after the rotation.\n- For any interior point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) we have \\(i-1\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n  \\[\n  (i-1,b)\\in T_{n},\n  \\]\n\n  because \\((i-1)+b\\le (i+b)-1\\le n\\). Hence the point \\((i-1,b)\\) is covered by the still‑present line \\(\\ell_{i-1}\\); consequently the vertical line \\(V_{i}\\) also contains \\((i,b)\\). Thus every point of the column remains covered.\n\nTherefore the family obtained from \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) by the substitution \\(\\ell_{i}\\mapsto V_{i}\\) still covers \\(T_{n}\\) and now has one fewer sunny line.\n\n---\n\n**(b) Horizontal replacement.**  \nChoose an index \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Replace \\(\\ell_{j}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe argument is symmetric to (a): the two endpoints of the row are also on neighbouring lines, and any interior point \\((a,j)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-j\\) is covered because \\((a,j+1)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{j+1}\\). Hence coverage is preserved and the sunny count drops by one.\n\n---\n\n**(c) Diagonal replacement.**  \nReplace the line \\(\\ell_{n}\\) (slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\)) by the genuine diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are still on \\(D\\). The two former endpoints \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) are also on the vertical line \\(V_{n-1}\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_{n-1}\\) respectively, which we may keep unchanged; consequently they remain covered. No other point loses a covering line, so the replacement is valid and reduces the sunny count by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\)\n\nStart from the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) (which has \\(k=n\\) sunny lines).  \nIf we wish to have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), perform \\(n-k\\) of the replacements described in §3, choosing for each replacement any line that has not yet been replaced. Because each replacement reduces the sunny count by exactly one and never destroys the covering property, after \\(n-k\\) steps we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* still covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of other values\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. The number of sunny lines is a non‑negative integer not exceeding the total number of lines, i.e. it must lie in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Since we have exhibited configurations for every integer in this set, no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
