rtical, and the line \\(x+y=0\\)) to “peel off’’ the outer layer of the triangle, thereby reducing the problem to a smaller triangle.\n\nCombining Lemma 1 with the construction in step 2 yields the claimed partial result.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\n*For each integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(n-m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) except for a prescribed set \\(R\\subseteq T_n\\) of exactly \\(m\\) points.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe argue by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nIf \\(m=0\\) we may take the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\).  \nIf \\(m=1\\) we delete any one of these lines; the uncovered point is the one that lay only on the deleted line.  \nIf \\(m=2\\) we keep a single line (say \\(x=1\\)); the two points not on it are the required uncovered set.  \nIf \\(m=3\\) we take \\(\\mathcal L_3=\\varnothing\\); all three points are uncovered.  \nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma true for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and all \\(0\\le m\\le n\\).  \nConsider \\(T_{n+1}\\). Its outer layer consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nObserve that \\(L\\) is a straight line of slope \\(-1\\); hence it is a non‑sunny line.  \nRemove this line from the plane. The remaining points form exactly the set \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet \\(m\\) be given with \\(0\\le m\\le n+1\\).  \nChoose a subset \\(R\\subseteq T_{n+1}\\) of size \\(m\\).  \nTwo cases arise.\n\n1. **\\(R\\) does not contain the whole line \\(L\