over all points of \\(S_{n}\\) each diagonal must contain at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) that meets it.\nIf \\(\\ell_{0}\\) meets a diagonal, that diagonal is already covered, leaving at most\n\\(n-1\\) diagonals for the non‑sunny lines, which is impossible because we have only\n\\(n-1\\) such lines.\nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{i}^{(y)}: y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2pt]\n&\\ell_{j}^{(x)}: x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2pt]\n&\\ell^{(d)}: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{(d)}\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a+b\\le n-1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{(x)}_{a}\\);  \nif \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{(y)}_{b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{1},\\alpha_{2},\\dots ,\\alpha_{k}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}: y=\\alpha_{i}x+\\beta_{i}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise non‑parallel and each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) meets exactly one diagonal\n\\(D_{m}\\) (this is always possible because a line of slope \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\) meets\neach diagonal in at most one point, and we may adjust the intercept \\(\\beta_{i}\\)\nto hit a prescribed diagonal).  \nAssign the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\) to the sunny lines,\none diagonal per line.\n\nAll remaining diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) contain only points not yet\ncovered.  For each such diagonal we use the same three families of non‑sunny\nlines as in the construction of Section 2:\n\n* for a diagonal \\(D_{m}\\) with \\(m\\le n\\) we cover its points by the vertical line\n  \\(x=m- (n-1)\\) (if the value lies between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\)) or by the horizontal\n  line \\(y=m- (n-1)\\);  \n* the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is covered by the line \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nBecause there are exactly \\(n-k\\) diagonals left, at most one non‑sunny line is\nrequired for each of them, giving at most \\(n-k\\) additional lines.\nTogether with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nand by construction every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nAll non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, while the\n\\(k\\) chosen lines are sunny.  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines exists for every \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n- No configuration with exactly one sunny line exists (Section 1).  \n- Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) (Section 2) and for every \\(k\\) with\n  \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) (Section 3).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self‑Correction Instruction**  \nThe solution above has been checked for logical completeness and adherence to the\nrequired format. All statements are proved, and the final answer is presented in\nthe prescribed boxed form."
