"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Set to be covered**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **A universal covering by \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 lines**  \n   For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-1\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   d_c:\\;y = x-c\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(1\\) (hence is *sunny*) and contains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) for which \\(a-b=c\\).  \n   Because for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(0\\le a-b\\le n-1\\), the family \\(\\{d_c\\mid 0\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Introducing a non\u2011sunny line**  \n   The vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v:\\;x=1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   is not sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  \n   The point \\((1,b)\\) for any admissible \\(b\\) lies on \\(v\\); consequently we may replace **one** of the diagonal lines by \\(v\\) without losing the covering property.\n\n4. **Replacing further diagonals by horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines**  \n   For each integer \\(h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-1\\) the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   h_h:\\;y=h\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   is also non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  \n   The line \\(h_h\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(h\\).\n\n5. **Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Define the family of \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,v\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical, non\u2011sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,h_h\\mid n-k\\le h\\le n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal, non\u2011sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,d_c\\mid 0\\le c\\le k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny diagonals}} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   - \\(v\\) contributes \\(1\\) line.  \n   - The horizontal block contributes \\((n-1)-(n-k)+1 = k\\) lines.  \n   - The diagonal block contributes \\(k\\) lines.  \n\n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = 1+k+k = n\\).  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) diagonal lines are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)**  \n\n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1: \\(a=1\\).*  \n   The point lies on the vertical line \\(v\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \n   Two sub\u2011cases are possible.\n\n   *Sub\u2011case\u202f2a: \\(b\\ge n-k\\).*  \n   The horizontal line \\(h_b\\) belongs to the second block of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in h_b\\).\n\n   *Sub\u2011case\u202f2b: \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).*  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a-b\\le n+1-2b .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b\\le n-k-1\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a-b\\le n+1-2b\\le n+1-2(n-k-1)=k .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(a\\ge b\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le 2b\\le 2(n-k-1)\\le n+1\\) would still hold, but the inequality \\(a-b\\ge0\\) is needed for the next step).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le a-b\\le k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The diagonal line \\(d_{\\,a-b}\\) belongs to the third block of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) (its offset lies in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)).  \n   By definition of \\(d_{\\,a-b}\\) we have \\(b = a-(a-b)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on that diagonal.\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n7. **Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\)**  \n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   Moreover, the point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\) (for any \\(n\\ge3\\)) is not on any diagonal line \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge0\\) because its first coordinate equals \\(1\\).  Consequently at least one