            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Set to be covered**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Diagonal (sunny) lines**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   d_c:\\;y = x-c .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are *sunny*) and contain precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) for which \\(a-b=c\\).  \n   Because for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(0\\le a-b\\le n-1\\), the family \\(\\{d_c\\mid 0\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Non‑sunny lines**  \n   *Vertical line* \\(v:\\;x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n   *Horizontal lines* \\(h_h:\\;y=h\\) for integers \\(h\\ge 1\\) (slope \\(0\\)).  \n   Both are not sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Define the following set of \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,v\\,\\}}_{\\text{one vertical, non‑sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,h_h\\mid 1\\le h\\le n-k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny horizontals}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,d_c\\mid 0\\le c\\le k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny diagonals}} .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   - The vertical block contributes \\(1\\) line.  \n   - The horizontal block contributes \\((n-k-1)-1+1 = n-k-1\\) lines (it is empty when \\(k=n-1\\)).  \n   - The diagonal block contributes \\(k\\) lines.  \n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L_k| = 1+(n-k-1)+k = n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) diagonal lines are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\)**  \n\n   Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a=1\\).*  \n   The point lies on the vertical line \\(v\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(b\\le n-1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - **Sub‑case 2a:** \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).  \n     The horizontal line \\(h_b\\) belongs to the second block of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in h_b\\).\n\n   - **Sub‑case 2b:** \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n     In this sub‑case we have \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and, since \\(b\\le n-1\\), we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     0\\le a-b \\le (n+1-b)-b = n+1-2b \\le n+1-2(n-k)=k-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     (The last inequality uses \\(b\\ge n-k\\).)  \n     Hence \\(a-b\\) is an integer belonging to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n     The diagonal line \\(d_{\\,a-b}\\) (which is in the third block) satisfies  \n\n     \\[\n     y = x-(a-b) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = a-(a-b),\n     \\]\n\n     so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(d_{\\,a-b}\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Thus the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n6. **Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\)**  \n\n   The point \\((1,2)\\in T_n\\) (valid for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)) cannot lie on any diagonal line \\(d_c\\) because all such lines have equation \\(y=x-c\\) and therefore contain the point \\((1,2)\\) only when \\(2=1-c\\), i.e. \\(c=-1\\), which is not allowed (\\(c\\