n-k\\)).  \n   - The number of sunny lines is precisely the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are\nexactly the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\nx+y=0\\;( \\text{equivalently }x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) the set \\(T_n\\) can be covered by\n\\(n-t\\) horizontal lines and one line of slope \\(1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-t).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-t\\) lies on one of\nthese lines.  \nThe points that remain have \\(b=n-t+1,\\dots ,n\\); for each such \\(b\\) the\nfirst coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).  Hence the remaining points are\n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n-t+i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(P_i\\) satisfy \\(y-x=n-t\\); consequently they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+(n-t),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a configuration contains \\(s\\) sunny lines, then by adding any\n\\(s-1\\) further distinct sunny lines we obtain a configuration with exactly\n\\(s\\) sunny lines and still \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe extra sunny lines can be chosen arbitrarily (for instance, through a\npoint already covered) because they do not affect the covering property.\nThey are distinct from the already existing lines, so the total number of\nsunny lines becomes \\(s\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  All of them are\nnon‑sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(t=k\\).  We obtain \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines and the sunny\nline  \n\n\\[\nL_k:\\;y=x+k .\n\\]\n\nThus we have a configuration with exactly one sunny line.  \nNow apply Lemma 2, adding \\(k-1\\) further distinct sunny lines (for example,\nthe lines \\(y=2x+1,\\;y=3x+2,\\dots ,y=kx+(k-1)\\) each passing through the\npoint \\((1,1)\\)).  The total number of lines is \\(n\\) and the number of sunny\nlines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered, because the original covering by\nthe \\(n-k\\) horizontals and \\(L_k\\) is unchanged, and the added sunny lines\ndo not remove any line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No other value of \\(k\\) is possible, because the\nnumber of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is\n\\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
