er with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and they\ncover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(t=k\\).  We obtain the following family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines:\n\n* the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the sunny lines \\(L_i:\\;y=x+(i-1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, and by Lemma 1 every point of \\(T_n\\) lies\non at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completeness of the answer\n\nThe number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is\n\\(n\\); therefore \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer in this interval is indeed\nrealizable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
