"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.  \nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \u2013 A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff its slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   \u2013 A line is *sunny* otherwise.  \n\n2. **The set to be covered.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|T_{n}|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (all non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n   Consequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\)).**  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) the line \\(L_{t}: y=x+t\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on \\(L_{t}\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces  \n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(L_{t}\\) contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) with intercepts \\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\):\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\;y = x + i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\]\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) exactly when it lies on one of the \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) is not on any \\(S_{i}\\) then \\(b-a\\le -1\\) or \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \n   In the first case \\(b\\le a-1