ne\n   \\[\n   V_{a}:\\;x=a .\n   \\]\n   These lines are non‑sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Removal of redundant vertical lines.*  \n   If all points of \\(T_{n}\\) having first coordinate \\(a\\) already belong to some sunny line, the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) is unnecessary and may be omitted.  \n   For a fixed \\(a\\) the possible second coordinates are\n   \\[\n   b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n   All these points are covered by the sunny family \\(\\{S_{i}\\}\\) iff every such \\(b\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. iff\n   \\[\n   a\\le b\\le a+k-1\\quad\\text{for all }b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n   This is equivalent to the two inequalities\n   \\[\n   1\\ge a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad n+1-a\\le a+k-1 .\n   \\]\n   The first forces \\(a=1\\); the second becomes \\(n\\le k\\).  \n   Consequently:\n\n   * If \\(k=n\\) then **every** point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on one of the sunny lines, and we may discard **all** vertical lines.\n   * If \\(k<n\\) then for **every** \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists at least one point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\). Hence no vertical line can be omitted.\n\n   Therefore the number of vertical lines that have to be kept is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   0 &\\text{if }k=n,\\\\[2mm]\n   n &\\text{if }k<n .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   *Total number of lines.*  \n   The total number of lines in the family is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{sunny}=k,\\qquad \n   \\#\\text{vertical}= \\begin{cases}\n   0 & (k=n),\\\\\n   n & (k<n),\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   which equals \\(n\\) in both cases.\n\n5. **Verification of the construction.**  \n\n   *Distinctness.*  The sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts, while the vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\); thus all lines are different.\n\n   *Sunny count.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); they are the only sunny lines.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   – If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{b-a}\\).  \n   – Otherwise \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); then the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (which is present unless \\(k=n\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   In the special case \\(k=n\\) the first alternative always holds, so every point is covered by a sunny line.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
