*Proof of Lemma 1.*  \n   Because \\((u,v)\\) is a primitive integer direction vector, every lattice\n   point of \\(\\ell\\) can be written uniquely as  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_{0},y_{0})+t\\,(u,v),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) is a fixed lattice point on \\(\\ell\\).  \n   For any two distinct points of \\(\\ell\\) belonging to \\(T_{n}\\) the\n   difference of their \\(x\\)-coordinates is a non‑zero multiple of \\(|u|\\),\n   and the difference of their \\(y\\)-coordinates is a non‑zero multiple of\n   \\(|v|\\).  Consequently the set of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates is an\n   arithmetic progression with step \\(|u|\\), and the set of admissible\n   \\(y\\)-coordinates is an arithmetic progression with step \\(|v|\\).\n\n   Since every point of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and\n   \\(1\\le y\\le n\\), the number of distinct values that an arithmetic\n   progression with step \\(|u|\\) can take inside the interval \\([1,n]\\) is at\n   most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|u|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The same bound holds with \\(|v|\\) in place of \\(|u|\\).  Because the line\n   is sunny we have \\(|u|\\ge 1,\\;|v|\\ge 1\\) and \\((u,v)\\neq (0,1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\);\n   in particular \\(|u|+|v|\\ge 3\\).  Hence at least one of \\(|u|,|v|\\) is\n   \\(\\ge 2\\), and from (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\bigl(\\ell\\cap T_{n}\\bigr)\\le\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   This proves Lemma 1. ∎\n\n4. **Impossibility of a sunny line in a feasible configuration.**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\n   requirement and contains at least one sunny line.\n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be such a sunny line.  \n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   Every line that is *not* sunny is parallel to one of the three forbidden\n   directions; each of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\)\n   (the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points, and the same bound holds\n   for the horizontal and the diagonal direction).\n\n   Hence, if the configuration contains exactly \\(s\\ge 1\\) sunny lines,\n   the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\,(n-1)+(n-s)\\,n = n^{2}-s \\le n^{2}-1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_{n}|\n   \\qquad\\text{(by elementary algebra).}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(n\\) lines could not cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\), contradicting\n   the hypothesis.  Therefore a feasible configuration cannot contain any\n   sunny line; i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction in step 2 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and the\n   argument in steps 3–4 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
